            "1.2",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.4",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-cg-brady-watt-ordered-spherical-root-complex",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be an irreducible Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, $n:=|S|\\ge1$, and let $\\alpha_i$, $R_i$, $c$, $h$, $(\\rho_i)$, $(\\mu_i)$, $\\mu$, $\\Phi_+$, $\\le_T$, $M$ and $t_\\alpha$ be as in [[def-cg-bipartite-coxeter-element-and-root-recursion]], [[lem-cg-steinberg-bipartite-root-enumeration]] and [[lem-cg-ordered-root-pairings-and-simple-systems]], so $\\Phi_+=\\{\\rho_1<\\rho_2<\\dots<\\rho_{nh/2}\\}$ in the global order. Write $R(\\alpha)$ for the reflection with normal $\\alpha$ and $S^{n-1}:=\\{x\\in V:B(x,x)=1\\}$.\n\n**(1) The complex $X(c)$.** Its vertex set is $\\Phi_+$. For $1\\le i<j\\le nh/2$, join $\\rho_i$ to $\\rho_j$ by an edge exactly when $R(\\rho_j)R(\\rho_i)\\le_Tc$. Let $X(c)$ contain the empty simplex and every finite nonempty set of vertices whose every two-element subset is an edge. Thus $X(c)$ is the abstract simplicial complex ([[def-abstract-simplicial-complex]]) determined by this ordered edge relation.\n\n**(2) The subcomplexes $X(\\sigma)$.** For $\\sigma\\le_Tc$, put $P_\\sigma:=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+:t_\\alpha\\le_T\\sigma\\}$ and let $X(\\sigma)$ be the full subcomplex of $X(c)$ on the vertex set $P_\\sigma$. For a positive root $\\rho$ in the global order, let $X(\\sigma,\\rho)$ be the full subcomplex on vertices in $P_\\sigma\\cap M(\\sigma)$ that are less than or equal to $\\rho$; thus $X(\\sigma,\\tau_i)$ has vertex set $\\{\\tau_1,\\dots,\\tau_i\\}$ when $P_\\sigma=\\{\\tau_1<\\dots<\\tau_t\\}$.\n\n**(3) Positive cones and realizations.** Every vertex is a unit vector. For a finite set $F$ of vertices define\n$$c[F]:=\\Bigl\\{\\sum_{v\\in F}\\lambda_vv:\\lambda_v\\ge0\\Bigr\\},\\qquad c[\\emptyset]:=\\{0\\}.$$\nFor a subcomplex $Y$ put $c[Y]:=\\bigcup_{F\\in Y}c[F]$ and $|Y|:=c[Y]\\cap S^{n-1}$, its positive-cone realization in the unit sphere. For $\\sigma\\le_Tc$ and a positive root $\\rho$, write\n$$Y(\\sigma,\\rho):=c[\\{\\tau\\in P_\\sigma\\cap M(\\sigma):\\tau\\le\\rho\\}].$$\n\n**(4) Abstentions.** This definition does not assert that $c[F]$ is nondegenerate for each simplex $F$, that $|F|$ is a spherical simplex, that the cone realization embeds $X(c)$ or any $X(\\sigma)$, or that these realizations are convex or have dimension $\\ell_T(\\sigma)-1$. It also does not assert an equivalence between higher simplex membership and a single full-tuple product condition. No Choice is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group with length $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$ and reflection set $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]]); every root has $B$-norm one.\n\n**(1) The root-reflection dictionary.** For $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ choose $w\\in W$, $s\\in S$ with $\\alpha=\\rho(w)e_s$ and put $t_\\alpha:=wsw^{-1}\\in T$. Then:\n(i) $t_\\alpha$ is independent of the chosen representation of $\\alpha$;\n(ii) $\\rho(t_\\alpha)=r_\\alpha$, the reflection with normal $\\alpha$; and $t_{\\rho(w)\\alpha}=w\\,t_\\alpha\\,w^{-1}$ for all $w\\in W$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$;\n(iii) $t_{-\\alpha}=t_\\alpha$, and for $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\Phi$ one has $t_\\alpha=t_\\beta$ if and only if $\\alpha=\\pm\\beta$;\n(iv) the induced map $\\{\\pm\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}\\to T$ is a bijection; so is the map $\\Phi_+\\to T$, $\\alpha\\mapsto t_\\alpha$.\n\n**(2) Inversion formula.** For every $w\\in W$ one has $|N(w)|=\\ell(w)$ (with $N$ as in [[def-cg-geometric-inversion-set]]); and for every reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_n$,\n$$N(w)=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_n)^{-1}e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},\\qquad N(w^{-1})=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_1\\cdots s_{i-1})e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},$$\nthe displayed elements being pairwise distinct positive roots.\n\n**(3) Strong exchange.** Let $w\\in W$ and $t\\in T$ satisfy $\\ell(tw)<\\ell(w)$, and let $w=s_1\\cdots s_n$ be a reduced expression. Then there is a unique $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ with\n$$tw=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_n,\\qquad t=r_i:=s_1\\cdots s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}\\cdots s_1;$$\nmoreover, if $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$ is the positive root with $t=t_\\alpha$, then $\\alpha\\in N(w^{-1})$ and $\\alpha=\\rho(s_1\\cdots s_{i-1})e_{s_i}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "2.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set and let $m$ be a Coxeter matrix on $S$, so that $m(s,s)=1$ and $m(s,t)=m(t,s)\\in\\{2,3,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ for $s\\ne t$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]).\n\n**(1) The space and its distinguished functions.** Let $V:=\\mathbb R^S$ be the real vector space of all functions $S\\to\\mathbb R$, with pointwise operations ([[def-function-space]]); it is a vector space over the ordered field $\\mathbb R$ ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]]). Write $u(s)$ for the value of $u$ at $s$ and put $e_s(s):=1$, $e_s(t):=0$ for $t\\ne s$.\n\n**(2) The Coxeter form.** For finite $m(s,t)$ put $c(s,t):=\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ ([[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]]) and for $m(s,t)=\\infty$ put $c(s,t):=1$; then $c(s,t)=c(t,s)$ and $c(s,s)=\\cos\\pi=-1$. The **Coxeter form** $B$ is the unique symmetric bilinear form on $V$ with $B(e_s,e_t)=-c(s,t)$ for all $s,t\\in S$; in particular $B(e_s,e_s)=1$ and $B(e_s,e_t)=-\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ for finite $m(s,t)$, while $B(e_s,e_t)=-1$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$ ([[def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms]], [[thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual]]). **No positive definiteness, definiteness or nondegeneracy of $B$ is presumed**: $\\operatorname{rad}(B):=\\{u:B(u,v)=0\\text{ for all }v\\in V\\}$ may be nonzero ([[def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form]]), and the form may be indefinite ([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]]). A linear map $g:V\\to V$ is **$B$-preserving** when $B(gu,gw)=B(u,w)$ for all $u,w\\in V$.\n\n**(3) Reflections.** For $a\\in V$ with $B(a,a)\\ne0$ define the **reflection with normal $a$** by $$r_a(v):=v-\\frac{2B(v,a)}{B(a,a)}a .$$ The definition asserts neither that $r_a$ is linear or an involution nor that $\\ker B(-,a)=\\{v:B(v,a)=0\\}$ is a hyperplane; these are proved in [[lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders]]. When $B(a,a)=0$ the symbol $r_a$ is not defined.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "2.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$, the reflections $r_a$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]]), the canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$, the root system $\\Phi=\\{\\rho(w)e_s\\}$, the reflections $T$, and the positive cone $V_+=\\{\\sum_s\\lambda_se_s:\\lambda_s\\ge0\\}$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]); every root satisfies $B(\\alpha,\\alpha)=1$ ([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]] (3)). Let $C,C^\\circ$ be the chamber and its interior of the dual action and put $B_s:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)>0\\}$ ([[def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes]]).\n\n**(1) Sign criterion for the cone.** For $v\\in V$: $v\\in V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$ if and only if $f(v)>0$ for every $f\\in C^\\circ$.\n\n**(2) Roots have a sign.** Every root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ lies in $V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$ or in $-V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and not in both. Hence, with\n$$\\Phi_+:=\\Phi\\cap V_+,\\qquad \\Phi_-:=\\Phi\\cap(-V_+),$$\none has $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$, $\\Phi_-=-\\Phi_+$, $e_s\\in\\Phi_+$ for every $s\\in S$, and for every $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$\n$$\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_+\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s,\\qquad \\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s.$$\nIn particular $f(\\alpha)>0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$, and $f(\\alpha)<0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_-$.\n\n**(3) Simple reflections act on positive roots.** For every $s\\in S$,\n$$r_s(\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\})=\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\},\\qquad r_se_s=-e_s,\\qquad r_s\\Phi=\\Phi;$$\nequivalently $r_s\\Phi_+=(\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\})\\cup\\{-e_s\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "2.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-cg-ordered-root-pairings-and-simple-systems",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be an irreducible Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite of cardinality $n\\ge2$, with the data $\\alpha_i$, $\\beta_i$, $s_i$, $R_i$, $a$, $b$, $c$, $h$, $(\\rho_i)$, $(\\mu_i)$ and the linear map $\\mu$ of [[def-cg-bipartite-coxeter-element-and-root-recursion]], and assume the conclusions of [[lem-cg-steinberg-bipartite-root-enumeration]] (so that $\\Phi_+=\\{\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_{nh/2}\\}$ and $\\mu(\\rho_i)=\\mu_i$). Let $\\ell_T$, $\\le_T$, $M$, $F$ be reflection length, absolute order and the moved and fixed spaces, and let $P_\\sigma:=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+:t_\\alpha\\le_T\\sigma\\}$ for $\\sigma\\le_Tc$, where $t_\\alpha\\in T$ is the reflection with normal $\\alpha$ ([[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1), [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]], [[thm-cg-carter-reflection-length-and-absolute-order]]). Then:\n\n**(1) The basic identities.** For every $i\\ge1$: $(c-\\mathrm{id})\\mu_i=-2\\rho_i$, $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_i=1$, and $\\mu_i\\in F(R(\\rho_i)c)$, i.e. $R(\\rho_i)c\\,\\mu_i=\\mu_i$; moreover $F(R(\\rho_i)c)$ is one dimensional and $\\mu_i$ is its unique vector satisfying $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_i=1$.\n\n**(2) Sign and vanishing identities.** (a) $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_j=-\\mu_{j+n}\\cdot\\rho_i$ for all $i,j\\ge1$; (b) $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_j\\ge0$ whenever $1\\le i\\le j\\le nh/2$; (c) $\\mu_{i+t}\\cdot\\rho_i=0$ for $1\\le t\\le n-1$ and all $i\\ge1$; (d) $\\mu_j\\cdot\\rho_i\\le0$ whenever $1\\le i<j\\le nh/2$.\n\n**(3) Separation from earlier cones.** If $1\\le i_1<i_2<\\dots<i_m<k\\le nh/2$, then $\\rho_k$ is not a nonnegative linear combination of $\\rho_{i_1},\\dots,\\rho_{i_m}$.\n\n**(4) Canonical simple systems of the subintervals.** Fix $\\sigma\\le_Tc$ with $\\sigma\\ne1$, put $k:=\\ell_T(\\sigma)=\\dim M(\\sigma)$ and write $P_\\sigma=\\{\\tau_1<\\tau_2<\\dots<\\tau_t\\}$ in the global $\\rho$-order. Then:\n\n(i) $P_\\sigma$ is the set of positive roots of the reflection subgroup $W_\\sigma:=\\{w\\in W:M(w)\\subseteq M(\\sigma)\\}$, and it contains a **simple system** $\\Delta=\\{\\delta_1,\\dots,\\delta_k\\}$: every root of $P_\\sigma$ is a nonnegative linear combination of the $\\delta$'s and $\\delta_1,\\dots,\\delta_k$ are linearly independent; moreover $\\delta_k=\\tau_t$ and, recursively, $\\delta_i$ is the last root of $P_\\sigma$ lying in $M(\\sigma R(\\delta_k)R(\\delta_{k-1})\\cdots R(\\delta_{i+1}))$; equivalently $\\delta_i$ is the first root of $P_\\sigma$ in $M(R(\\delta_{i-1})\\cdots R(\\delta_1)\\sigma)$, with the product empty for $i=1$, so in particular $\\delta_1=\\tau_1$.\n\n(ii) $\\varepsilon_i:=R(\\delta_1)R(\\delta_2)\\cdots R(\\delta_{i-1})\\delta_i$ is a positive root for each $i$, and\n\n$$\\sigma=R(\\varepsilon_k)R(\\varepsilon_{k-1})\\cdots R(\\varepsilon_1),\\qquad \\sigma=R(\\varepsilon_i)\\,R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_{i-1})R(\\delta_{i+1})\\cdots R(\\delta_k).$$\n\n(iii) Let $\\mathcal S_\\sigma:=\\operatorname{cone}\\{\\delta_1,\\dots,\\delta_k\\}\\cap S(M(\\sigma))$ be the spherical simplex, where $S(M(\\sigma))=\\{x\\in M(\\sigma):B(x,x)=1\\}$. Its closed spherical wall opposite $\\delta_i$ is\n$$\\operatorname{cone}\\{\\delta_j:j\\ne i\\}\\cap S(M(\\sigma))=\\mathcal S_\\sigma\\cap \\mu(\\varepsilon_i)^\\perp,$$\nand its supporting hyperplane in $M(\\sigma)$ is $M(\\sigma)\\cap\\mu(\\varepsilon_i)^\\perp$.\n\n(iv) If $i<j$ and $\\varepsilon_i>\\varepsilon_j$ in the global order, then $\\varepsilon_i\\cdot\\varepsilon_j=0$, so $R(\\varepsilon_i)$ and $R(\\varepsilon_j)$ commute; the reordering $\\theta_1<\\theta_2<\\dots<\\theta_k$ of $\\varepsilon_1,\\dots,\\varepsilon_k$ in the global order satisfies $\\sigma=R(\\theta_k)R(\\theta_{k-1})\\cdots R(\\theta_1)$ and is lexicographically first among all increasing $k$-tuples $\\eta_1<\\cdots<\\eta_k$ in $P_\\sigma$ for which $R(\\eta_k)\\cdots R(\\eta_1)\\le_Tc$ and $\\ell_T(R(\\eta_k)\\cdots R(\\eta_1))=k$.\n\n(v) If $\\ell_T(\\sigma)=2$ then $\\{\\tau_1,\\tau_t\\}$ is a simple system of $P_\\sigma$, the only factorizations of $\\sigma$ as a product of two reflections in $W$ are $\\sigma=R(\\tau_1)R(\\tau_t)=R(\\tau_2)R(\\tau_1)=\\dots=R(\\tau_t)R(\\tau_{t-1})$, and $\\tau_1\\cdot\\tau_t\\le0$ while $\\tau_i\\cdot\\tau_{i+1}\\ge0$ for $1\\le i<t$; dually, for distinct positive roots $\\rho_i,\\rho_j$ with $R(\\rho_i)R(\\rho_j)\\le_Tc$ one has: if $i<j$ then $\\rho_i\\cdot\\rho_j\\le0$, and if $i>j$ then $\\rho_i\\cdot\\rho_j\\ge0$.\n\n(vi) Whenever $\\tau_{i_1}<\\dots<\\tau_{i_k}$ is an increasing tuple of roots of $P_\\sigma$ for which $R(\\tau_{i_k})\\cdots R(\\tau_{i_1})\\le_Tc$ and $\\ell_T(R(\\tau_{i_k})\\cdots R(\\tau_{i_1}))=k$, one has $\\tau_{i_j}\\ge\\theta_j$ for every $j=1,\\dots,k$.\n\nNo Choice is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-cg-ordered-root-complex-is-geometric-simplicial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "With the notation of [[def-cg-brady-watt-ordered-spherical-root-complex]] and the conclusions of [[lem-cg-ordered-root-pairings-and-simple-systems]]:\n\n**(1) Factorization criterion.** For any strictly increasing tuple $a_1<a_2<\\dots<a_k$ of roots in $\\Phi_+$, including the empty tuple when $k=0$,\n$$\\ell_T\\bigl(R(a_1)R(a_2)\\cdots R(a_k)c\\bigr)=n-k\\iff \\mu(a_i)\\cdot a_j=0\\quad\\text{for every }i>j.$$\n\n**(2) Linear independence and spherical simplices.** If $F=\\{a_1<\\dots<a_k\\}$ is a nonempty simplex of $X(c)$, then $a_1,\\dots,a_k$ are linearly independent and lie in a common open halfspace, namely $\\{x:B(x,f)>0\\}$ for every $f\\in\\mathcal C^\\circ$. Thus $c[F]$ is a pointed simplicial cone and $|F|:=c[F]\\cap S^{n-1}$ is a spherical simplex of dimension $k-1$. The empty face has $c[\\emptyset]=\\{0\\}$ and $|\\emptyset|=\\emptyset$. For any increasing tuple of positive roots, its set is a simplex of $X(c)$ if and only if its reverse product $R(a_k)\\cdots R(a_1)$ lies below $c$ in absolute order and has reflection length $k$.\n\n**(3) The complex structure and its dimension.** For every $\\sigma\\le_Tc$, the full subcomplex $X(\\sigma)$ is a finite simplicial complex of dimension $\\ell_T(\\sigma)-1$; in particular $X(1)=\\{\\emptyset\\}$ has dimension $-1$. Each $X(\\sigma,\\rho)$ is a simplicial complex. If $P_\\sigma=\\{\\tau_1<\\dots<\\tau_t\\}$, then\n$$X(\\sigma,\\tau_i)\\subseteq X(\\sigma,\\tau_{i+1})\\quad(1\\le i<t),$$\nand the simplices of $X(\\sigma,\\tau_{i+1})$ not already in $X(\\sigma,\\tau_i)$ are exactly the cones $B\\cup\\{\\tau_{i+1}\\}$ over faces $B$ of $X(\\sigma,\\tau_i)$ whose vertices all lie in $\\mu(\\tau_{i+1})^\\perp$. The empty face is allowed as a base, giving the new singleton vertex.\n\n**(4) Geometric intersections are common faces.** For any two faces $F,F'$ of $X(\\sigma)$,\n$$c[F]\\cap c[F']=c[F\\cap F'].$$\nConsequently, the normalized cone map from the ordinary geometric realization of $X(\\sigma)$ to $S^{n-1}$ is an embedding onto $|X(\\sigma)|$, and this image is a finite union of spherical simplices that pairwise meet in common faces. No Choice is used.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "def-abstract-simplicial-complex",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **abstract simplicial complex** is a pair $(V,K)$ consisting of a set $V$ of\nvertices and a family $K$ of finite subsets of $V$ such that:\n\n1. $\\varnothing \\in K$;\n2. if $\\sigma \\in K$ and $\\tau \\subseteq \\sigma$, then $\\tau \\in K$;\n3. every singleton $\\{v\\}$ with $v \\in V$ lies in $K$.\n\nThe elements of $K$ are the **simplices** of the complex. If $\\sigma \\in K$ is\nnonempty, its dimension is $\\dim \\sigma := |\\sigma|-1$, and the empty simplex\nhas dimension $-1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-rank-nullity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map of vector spaces over $F$, with $V$\nfinite-dimensional. Then\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\operatorname{nullity}T+\\operatorname{rank}T.$$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\dim_F(\\ker T)+\\dim_F(\\operatorname{im}T).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb F$ be $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, with **conjugation** $z\\mapsto\\overline z$\nthe identity on $\\mathbb R$ and complex conjugation on $\\mathbb C$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]). A **real** or\n**complex inner-product space** is an $\\mathbb F$-vector space $V$ together with\nan inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb F$ in the sense\nof [[def-inner-product-space]]: for all $u,v,w\\in V$ and $a,b\\in\\mathbb F$,\n\n$$\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle=a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle u,v\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle v,u\\rangle},\\qquad \\langle v,v\\rangle\\ge0,\\quad \\langle v,v\\rangle=0\\iff v=0 .$$\n\n**Conjugate-linearity in the second argument.** Conjugate symmetry together with\nlinearity in the first argument gives, for all $w\\in V$,\n\n$$\\langle w,au+bv\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle}=\\overline{a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle}=\\overline a\\,\\langle w,u\\rangle+\\overline b\\,\\langle w,v\\rangle .$$\n\n**Induced length.** The **induced length**, or inner-product norm, of $v$ is\n\n$$\\|v\\|:=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is exactly the published [[def-inner-product-norm]]: positive definiteness\nmakes $\\langle v,v\\rangle$ a nonnegative real with a unique nonnegative square\nroot, so that $\\|v\\|\\ge0$, $\\|v\\|=0$ exactly for $v=0$, and\n$\\langle v,v\\rangle=\\|v\\|^2$.\n\n**The convention on this page.** Every inner-product space below is real or\ncomplex in this sense, the pairing is linear in the first argument and\nconjugate-linear in the second, and $\\|v\\|$ always denotes the induced length.\nIn the real case conjugation is the identity and $|z|$ is the absolute value of\n$\\mathbb R$; in the complex case they are complex conjugation and the complex\nmodulus. All statements below are therefore written once and read in either\nscalar field.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.3",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$, the reflections $r_a$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]]), the canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$, the root system $\\Phi=\\{\\rho(w)e_s\\}$, the reflections $T$, and the positive cone $V_+=\\{\\sum_s\\lambda_se_s:\\lambda_s\\ge0\\}$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]); every root satisfies $B(\\alpha,\\alpha)=1$ ([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]] (3)). Let $C,C^\\circ$ be the chamber and its interior of the dual action and put $B_s:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)>0\\}$ ([[def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes]]).\n\n**(1) Sign criterion for the cone.** For $v\\in V$: $v\\in V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$ if and only if $f(v)>0$ for every $f\\in C^\\circ$.\n\n**(2) Roots have a sign.** Every root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ lies in $V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$ or in $-V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and not in both. Hence, with\n$$\\Phi_+:=\\Phi\\cap V_+,\\qquad \\Phi_-:=\\Phi\\cap(-V_+),$$\none has $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$, $\\Phi_-=-\\Phi_+$, $e_s\\in\\Phi_+$ for every $s\\in S$, and for every $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$\n$$\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_+\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s,\\qquad \\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s.$$\nIn particular $f(\\alpha)>0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$, and $f(\\alpha)<0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_-$.\n\n**(3) Simple reflections act on positive roots.** For every $s\\in S$,\n$$r_s(\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\})=\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\},\\qquad r_se_s=-e_s,\\qquad r_s\\Phi=\\Phi;$$\nequivalently $r_s\\Phi_+=(\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\})\\cup\\{-e_s\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "**Inversion inside a subinterval.** If $n=1$, (1) is vacuous because $P_\\sigma$ has only its single positive root. Assume $n\\ge2$. Fix $u\\le_Tc$, $u\\ne1$, and let $\\Delta_u=\\{d_1,\\dots,d_m\\}$ be the simple system of $P_u$ given by [F3]. The item-18 factorization, in its second form with index $1$, is $u=R(d_1)\\cdots R(d_m)$; it is reduced because $\\ell_T(u)=m$. Apply the inversion formula [F4] to the finite Coxeter system $W_u$ with simple system $\\Delta_u$. Its positive roots are exactly $P_u$, so $\\{\\alpha\\in P_u:u^{-1}\\alpha\\in-P_u\\}=\\{\\varepsilon_1,\\dots,\\varepsilon_m\\}=\\{\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_m\\}$, where the prefix roots are $\\varepsilon_j=R(d_1)\\cdots R(d_{j-1})d_j$. The action of $u$ preserves $M(u)$, so a root in $M(u)$ is negative in this subsystem exactly when it is negative in the ambient positive system. Thus for $\\alpha\\in P_u\\setminus\\{\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_m\\}$, $u^{-1}\\alpha\\in P_u$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "**The first separator.** Write $s=R(\\tau_s)$ and $a=R(\\tau_a)$. Since $B(\\mu(\\tau_s),\\tau_a)=0$, [F2] and [F6] give $\\tau_a\\in M(sc)$ and hence $a\\le_Tsc$. The distinct reflections $s,a$ have product determinant $+1$ and nonidentity image, whereas every reflection has determinant $-1$; hence $\\ell_T(sa)=2$. Writing $sc=az$ gives $c=saz$ with $2+\\ell_T(z)=\\ell_T(c)$, so $sa\\le_Tc$. Its moved space is contained in $M(\\sigma)$ because both normals lie there, hence [F5] gives $sa\\le_T\\sigma$. The rank-two statement [F10] applied to $sa$ shows that $\\tau_s$ and $\\tau_a$ are the ordered endpoints of the simple system of $P_{sa}$. Put $\\tau_b:=R(\\tau_a)\\tau_s=\\tau_s-2B(\\tau_s,\\tau_a)\\tau_a$. The endpoint pairing in [F10] makes this a nonzero nonnegative combination of the endpoints; [F9] says it is a root, and [F15] makes it positive because its simple-root coordinates are nonnegative. The reflection $R(\\tau_a)$ preserves $M(sa)$, so $\\tau_b\\in P_{sa}$. The endpoints are the least and greatest roots of $P_{sa}$, hence $\\tau_s\\le\\tau_b\\le\\tau_a$; equality $\\tau_b=\\tau_a$ would imply $\\tau_s=-\\tau_a$, so $\\tau_b<\\tau_a$. Thus $\\tau_b\\in P_\\sigma$ as $sa\\le_T\\sigma$. The pair $(\\tau_b,\\tau_a)$ has reverse product $R(\\tau_a)R(\\tau_b)=sa\\le_Tc$ of length $2$, so [F7] gives $B(\\mu(\\tau_a),\\tau_b)=0$. Also $B(\\mu(\\tau_s),\\tau_b)=B(\\mu(\\tau_s),\\tau_s-2B(\\tau_s,\\tau_a)\\tau_a)=1>0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "**The linear map and finite facet test.** Regard $C=\\rho(c)$ as an orthogonal operator. Since $C-I$ is invertible, $\\mu=2(I-C)^{-1}$ and $\\mu+\\mu^*=2(I-C)^{-1}+2(I-C^{-1})^{-1}=2I$, because $(I-C^{-1})^{-1}=-(I-C)^{-1}C$. Thus for every unit root $x$, $B(\\mu(x),x)=1$. We will use the following finite polyhedral observation at each induction stage: if a full-dimensional cone in a finite-dimensional space is given by finitely many homogeneous linear inequalities, delete the redundant inequalities. For each remaining inequality $g\\ge0$, nonredundancy gives a point satisfying the other inequalities but with $g<0$; join it to an interior point where every remaining inequality is strict. The segment meets $g=0$ while all other inequalities remain strict, so $g=0$ cuts out a facet. Conversely each facet has a relative-interior point where at least one defining inequality is active, and that active hyperplane contains the facet. Hence the cone is the intersection of its active facet halfspaces. This proves the finite facet test without treating a redundant constraint as a facet.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F14"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "The mu pairing functionals restricted to M(sigma) are dual to Delta, giving prefix inclusions and the direct rank-one base.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "**The opposite separator.** Set $\\tau_c:=\\sigma^{-1}\\tau_s$. By [F1], $\\tau_s\\in P_\\sigma$; the hypothesis excludes the inversion roots in [F3], so [F4] and step 1.1 imply $\\tau_c\\in P_\\sigma$. Put $t:=R(\\tau_s)$ and $u:=t\\sigma$. Write $\\sigma=tu_0$ with additive reflection lengths, so $u=u_0$ and $u_0^{-1}\\sigma=u_0^{-1}tu_0$ has length $1$; hence $u\\le_T\\sigma$. If $c=\\sigma v$ is reduced, then $tc=u_0v$ and $\\ell_T(tc)=n-1=\\ell_T(u_0)+\\ell_T(v)$ because $t\\le_T\\sigma\\le_Tc$, so also $u\\le_Ttc$. The vector $\\mu(\\tau_s)$ is fixed by $tc$ by [F2], hence by $u$ using [F5]-[F6]. Let $p$ be the orthogonal projection of $\\mu(\\tau_s)$ onto $M(\\sigma)$. Its residual lies in $F(\\sigma)\\subseteq F(u)$, so $p\\in F(u)$ as well; projection preserves its pairing with $\\tau_s\\in M(\\sigma)$, giving $B(p,\\tau_s)=1$. Therefore $\\sigma p=tp=p-2\\tau_s$. Since $\\sigma\\tau_c=\\tau_s$ and $\\sigma$ is an isometry, $B(\\mu(\\tau_s),\\tau_c)=B(p,\\tau_c)=B(\\sigma p,\\tau_s)=B(p-2\\tau_s,\\tau_s)=-1$. By [F2], if $\\tau_s<\\tau_c$ then this pairing is nonnegative, and equality of the roots would give $1$; thus $\\tau_c<\\tau_s<\\tau_a$. Since $s\\sigma=\\sigma R(\\tau_c)$ and $sa\\le_T\\sigma$, one has $a\\le_Ts\\sigma=\\sigma R(\\tau_c)$. Write $s\\sigma=az$ with $\\ell_T(z)=k-2$. Then $\\sigma=azR(\\tau_c)$ and $(aR(\\tau_c))^{-1}\\sigma=R(\\tau_c)zR(\\tau_c)$ has length $k-2$ by conjugation invariance. The distinct reflections $a$ and $R(\\tau_c)$ have product of determinant $+1$ and this product is nonidentity, so its reflection length is $2$; the length equality therefore gives $aR(\\tau_c)\\le_T\\sigma\\le_Tc$. Now [F7] gives $B(\\mu(\\tau_a),\\tau_c)=0$. Together with step 1.2 this proves (1).",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "**Base of the root-index induction.** Put $\\sigma_0:=R(\\theta_k)\\sigma=R(\\theta_{k-1})\\cdots R(\\theta_1)$. The prefix $\\{\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_{k-1}\\}$ is a face of the canonical $k$-simplex by [F7], so $\\sigma_0\\le_Tc$ and $\\ell_T(\\sigma_0)=k-1$. The tuple $\\theta_1<\\dots<\\theta_{k-1}$ is eligible for $\\sigma_0$, so [F3]'s componentwise minimality makes its canonical tuple $\\theta'_1<\\dots<\\theta'_{k-1}$ satisfy $\\theta'_j\\le\\theta_j$ for every $j$. In particular $\\theta'_{k-1}<\\theta_k$, and appending $\\theta_k$ to this tuple gives an increasing reduced tuple for $\\sigma$; componentwise minimality for $\\sigma$ gives $\\theta_j\\le\\theta'_j$ for $j<k$. Thus $\\theta'_j=\\theta_j$ for every $j<k$. Also $M(\\sigma_0)=\\operatorname{span}(\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_{k-1})$. The dual-basis result of step 1.4 makes $B(\\mu(\\theta_k),\\tau)\\ge0$ for every $\\tau\\in P_\\sigma$, while root order makes this pairing nonpositive when $\\tau<\\theta_k$ by [F2]; hence every such $\\tau$ lies in $M(\\sigma)\\cap\\mu(\\theta_k)^\\perp$. This hyperplane has dimension $k-1$ and contains the independent roots $\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_{k-1}$, so $M(\\sigma_0)=M(\\sigma)\\cap\\mu(\\theta_k)^\\perp$. Since $\\theta_{k-1}<\\theta_k=\\tau_{i_0}$, the prefix ending at $\\tau_{i_0-1}$ is eligible for $\\sigma_0$. Every $\\tau_j<\\theta_k$ has $B(\\mu(\\theta_k),\\tau_j)\\ge0$ by the dual-basis result above and $\\le0$ by the global order, so these earlier roots lie in $M(\\sigma_0)=M(\\sigma)\\cap\\mu(\\theta_k)^\\perp$. Conversely $P_{\\sigma_0}\\subseteq P_\\sigma$ by [F3]. Thus the two prefix vertex sets, and hence their full subcomplexes, agree: $F_0:=X(\\sigma,\\tau_{i_0-1})=X(\\sigma_0,\\tau_{i_0-1})$. The lower-rank induction gives $c[F_0]=Z(\\sigma_0,\\tau_{i_0-1})$, a full-dimensional convex cone in $L_0:=M(\\sigma_0)$ with facet supports restricted from $\\mu(b)^\\perp$ for roots $b\\in P_{\\sigma_0}$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "step 1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "Inside E=M(sigma), the cone V_a=F+R_{>=0}a has a finite transported facet presentation whose side supports are mu-root hyperplanes.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F9",
            "F14",
            "step 1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-4",
          "claim": "The link base is the eligible subsystem prefix ending at its last root b below the ambient cutoff; lower-rank induction makes its cone full-dimensional and convex.",
          "step": "2.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F13",
            "F15",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "**Initial cone and its facets.** Let $K_{i_0}:=c[X(\\sigma,\\theta_k)]$. By [F7], $K_{i_0}=c[F_0]+\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\theta_k$, so it is a full-dimensional convex cone. The base support is $\\mu(\\theta_k)^\\perp$; each other facet has support $\\mu(r)^\\perp$ for a positive root $r\\in P_\\sigma$ by step 2.3. It contains the apex, so $B(\\mu(r),\\theta_k)=0$. If $r<\\theta_k$ and $r\\notin\\{\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_k\\}$, step 1.1 gives two roots of $F_0$ on opposite sides of $\\mu(r)^\\perp$, contradicting that it supports the cone. Hence every side facet is labelled by a $\\theta_j$ or a root $r>\\theta_k$. The $\\theta_j$ inequalities have positive sign on $K_{i_0}$ by [F3], the base apex inequality is positive on $\\theta_k$ and zero on $F_0$, and each later-root inequality is nonpositive on all vertices of the prefix. The finite facet test of step 1.3 therefore gives $Z(\\sigma,\\theta_k)\\subseteq K_{i_0}$. With the reverse inclusion from step 1.4, equality holds at $i_0$; the same facet argument records that any other listed halfspace not defining one of these facets is redundant.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "The new prefix is $X(\\sigma,\\tau_{i+1})=X(\\sigma,\\tau_i)\\cup(a*F)$ by [F7], so its cone is $K_{i+1}=K_i\\cup V$ with $V=c[F]+\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}a$. Apply step 2.3 to each facet of $F$; every facet of $V$ is supported by $\\mu(a)^\\perp$ or by $\\mu(r)^\\perp$ for $r\\in P_\\sigma$. Each side facet contains $a$, so $B(\\mu(r),a)=0$. If $r<a$ and $r$ is not a $\\theta$-root, step 1.1 produces two vertices of $F$ on opposite sides of $\\mu(r)^\\perp$, contradicting support. Thus every facet label is a $\\theta_j$, $a$, or a root $r>a$. Its containing halfspace is respectively $\\mu(\\theta_j)^+$, $\\mu(a)^+$, or $\\mu(r)^-$, by the sign inequalities in [F2]-[F3]. The cone $Z(\\sigma,\\tau_{i+1})\\cap\\mu(a)^+$ satisfies all these facet halfspaces; step 1.3 therefore puts it in $V$. Its other half $Z(\\sigma,\\tau_{i+1})\\cap\\mu(a)^-$ equals $Z(\\sigma,\\tau_i)=K_i$. Consequently $Z(\\sigma,\\tau_{i+1})\\subseteq K_{i+1}$. The reverse containment follows from step 1.4, proving $K_{i+1}=Z(\\sigma,\\tau_{i+1})$. The active-facet list is a subset of the defining inequalities; any omitted or repeated inequality is recorded as redundant.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 2.3",
            "step 2.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "**Endpoint.** The induction ends at $i=t$, where $Y(\\sigma,\\tau_t)=c[P_\\sigma]$. Thus the endpoint equality gives both the full positive-root cone and the claimed intersection with $M(\\sigma)$ and the $\\mu(\\theta_j)^+$ halfspaces.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "**Spherical convexity.** The cone $Z(\\sigma,\\tau_t)$ is convex and lies in the common open halfspace of the positive roots by [F7]. For unit vectors $x,y$ in its sphere section, write $\\phi=\\arccos B(x,y)<\\pi$. If $\\phi=0$ the arc is constant. Otherwise put $e=(y-\\cos\\phi\\,x)/\\sin\\phi$, so $B(e,x)=0$ and $B(e,e)=1$. The shorter great-circle arc is $\\gamma(s)=\\cos(s)x+\\sin(s)e=\\frac{\\sin(\\phi-s)}{\\sin\\phi}x+\\frac{\\sin s}{\\sin\\phi}y$ for $0\\le s\\le\\phi$. Its coefficients are nonnegative, so $\\gamma(s)$ lies in the convex cone and has unit norm. This proves spherical convexity.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F14",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Since $X(\\alpha)$ and $X(\\beta)$ are full subcomplexes of the finite complex $X(c)$, every face cone of their intersection is a common face. If a point belongs to both $c[X(\\alpha)]$ and $c[X(\\beta)]$, it lies in cones $c[F]$ and $c[F']$ for faces in the respective subcomplexes; [F11] identifies their intersection with $c[F\\cap F']$, which lies in the cone of the common subcomplex. Intersecting the resulting cone equality with $S^{n-1}$ proves the realization identity in (3). All inductions are finite and use no Choice. The theorem does not assert a meet of moved spaces or the lattice property of $[1,c]$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
