    "ex-cg-ordered-roots-and-mu-matrix-in-a3": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(2) The Coxeter form.** For finite $m(s,t)$ put $c(s,t):=\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ ([[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]]) and for $m(s,t)=\\infty$ put $c(s,t):=1$; then $c(s,t)=c(t,s)$ and $c(s,s)=\\cos\\pi=-1$. The **Coxeter form** $B$ is the unique symmetric bilinear form on $V$ with $B(e_s,e_t)=-c(s,t)$ for all $s,t\\in S$; in particular $B(e_s,e_s)=1$ and $B(e_s,e_t)=-\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ for finite $m(s,t)$, while $B(e_s,e_t)=-1$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$ ([[def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms]], [[thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual]]). **No positive definiteness, definiteness or nondegeneracy of $B$ is presumed**: $\\operatorname{rad}(B):=\\{u:B(u,v)=0\\text{ for all }v\\in V\\}$ may be nonzero ([[def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form]]), and the form may be indefinite ([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]]). A linear map $g:V\\to V$ is **$B$-preserving** when $B(gu,gw)=B(u,w)$ for all $u,w\\in V$.\n\n**(3) Reflections.** For $a\\in V$ with $B(a,a)\\ne0$ define the **reflection with normal $a$** by $$r_a(v):=v-\\frac{2B(v,a)}{B(a,a)}a .$$ The definition asserts neither that $r_a$ is linear or an involution nor that $\\ker B(-,a)=\\{v:B(v,a)=0\\}$ is a hyperplane; these are proved in [[lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders]]. When $B(a,a)=0$ the symbol $r_a$ is not defined.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Relators and descent.** For every $s\\in S$ one has $r_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_V$, and for all $s\\ne t$ with $m(s,t)<\\infty$ one has $(r_sr_t)^{m(s,t)}=\\mathrm{id}_V$. Consequently the assignment $s\\mapsto r_s$ sends every relator of the presentation to the identity, and by the universal property of [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]] there is a **unique** group homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ with $\\rho(s)=r_s$ for all $s\\in S$.\n\n**(2) $\\rho$ preserves the form.** For every $w\\in W$ and all $u,w'\\in V$, $B(\\rho(w)u,\\rho(w)w')=B(u,w')$.\n\n**(3) Roots have unit norm.** For every $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$, $B(\\rho(w)e_s,\\rho(w)e_s)=1$; hence every root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ satisfies $B(\\alpha,\\alpha)=1\\ne0$ and $r_\\alpha$ is defined.\n\n**(4) Conjugation of reflections.** Let $g\\in\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ be $B$-preserving and let $a\\in V$ with $B(a,a)\\ne0$. Then $gr_ag^{-1}=r_{ga}$. In particular, for every $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$, $$\\rho(wsw^{-1})=\\rho(w)r_s\\rho(w)^{-1}=r_{\\rho(w)e_s},$$ so every $t=wsw^{-1}\\in T$ acts on $V$ as the reflection in the root $\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix with presented group $W$, length $\\ell$ and geometric representation $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ as in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots]] and [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]], and let $J\\subseteq S$.\n\n1. **Support and reduction.** For every $w\\in W$ the set $S(w)$ of letters occurring in a reduced expression of $w$ is independent of the reduced expression, and every word in $S$ representing $w$ can be transformed into a reduced expression by repeatedly deleting two letters (Tits reduction, using only letters already present). Consequently\n$$W_J=\\langle J\\rangle=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq J\\}.$$\n2. **Intrinsic parabolic presentation.** Let $W_J^\\ast$ be the group presented by the restricted Coxeter matrix $(J,m|_J)$ in the sense of [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]. The canonical homomorphism $W_J^\\ast\\to W_J$, $s\\mapsto s$, is an isomorphism. Hence $(W_J,J)$ is a Coxeter system, its intrinsic length function $\\ell_J$ agrees with the ambient length $\\ell$ on $W_J$, and $W_J\\cap S=J$.\n3. **Minimal coset representatives.** Every left coset $W_J a:=\\{ua:u\\in W_J\\}$ ($a\\in W$, [[def-coset]]) has a unique element $d$ of minimal length; it is characterized by $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$, and it satisfies\n$$\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)\\qquad\\text{for all }u\\in W_J .$$\nEquivalently, every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_J$ and $d$ the minimal representative of the left coset $W_J d$, and then $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$. By inversion ($w\\mapsto w^{-1}$ preserves lengths and interchanges the two coset families $\\{W_Ja\\}$ and $\\{aW_J\\}$), every right coset $aW_J:=\\{au:u\\in W_J\\}$ has a unique minimal element $d$, characterized by $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$ and satisfying $\\ell(du)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(u)$ for all $u\\in W_J$.\n4. **Type A.** Let $n\\ge2$, $S=\\{s_1,\\dots,s_{n-1}\\}$ and $m(s_i,s_j):=3$ if $|i-j|=1$, $m(s_i,s_j):=2$ if $|i-j|>1$ (a Coxeter matrix of type $A_{n-1}$). Then $s_i\\mapsto(i\\ i+1)$ extends to an isomorphism $W\\to S_n$ (the letters $1,\\dots,n$ carry the library's symmetric group by the order-preserving identification with $\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$, under which $(i\\ i+1)$ is the adjacent transposition $(i-1\\ i)$), and for every $w\\in W$,\n$$\\ell(w)=\\operatorname{inv}\\bigl(\\varphi(w)\\bigr),$$\nthe inversion number of the corresponding permutation ([[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]]). In particular a word in the $s_i$ is reduced if and only if its length equals the inversion number of its value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cg-bipartite-coxeter-element-and-root-recursion",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(2) Cyclic indexing.** Read subscripts $i$ of $s_i$, $\\alpha_i$, $R_i$ and $\\beta_i$ cyclically modulo $n$: $s_{i+n}:=s_i$, $\\alpha_{i+n}:=\\alpha_i$, $R_{i+n}:=R_i$, and $\\beta_{i+n}:=\\beta_i$ for the dual family below. The cyclic indexing of the $\\beta_i$ is part of the convention: it is what makes the vector $\\mu_i$ below well defined for every $i\\ge1$ (see (3)).\n\n**(3) Prefix roots and dual vertices.** Let $G=(B(\\alpha_j,\\alpha_k))_{j,k=1}^n$ be the Gram matrix. It is invertible: for $x\\ne0$, the basis property gives $\\sum_jx_j\\alpha_j\\ne0$, so $x^{\\mathsf T}Gx=B(\\sum_jx_j\\alpha_j,\\sum_jx_j\\alpha_j)>0$. Set $\\beta_i:=\\sum_{k=1}^n(G^{-1})_{ki}\\alpha_k$; symmetry of $G$ gives $B(\\beta_i,\\alpha_j)=\\sum_kG_{jk}(G^{-1})_{ki}=\\delta_{ji}$. These vectors are unique, since a vector orthogonal to every basis vector is orthogonal to itself and hence is zero by positive definiteness. Thus $(\\beta_1,\\dots,\\beta_n)$ is the $B$-dual family of $(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_n)$. **Define**, for every integer $i\\ge1$,\n\n$$\\rho_i:=R_1R_2\\cdots R_{i-1}\\,\\alpha_i,\\qquad \\mu_i:=R_1R_2\\cdots R_{i-1}\\,\\beta_i,$$\n\nthe empty product for $i=1$ being the identity. Put $c_V:=\\rho(c)\\in\\mathrm{GL}(V)$. The recursions $\\rho_{i+n}=c_V\\rho_i$ and $\\mu_{i+n}=c_V\\mu_i$ (valid for all $i\\ge1$) follow by separating the first $n$ factors and using cyclic indexing; they are also recorded in [[lem-cg-steinberg-bipartite-root-enumeration]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-cg-steinberg-bipartite-root-enumeration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(3) Enumeration of the roots.** Every $\\rho_i$ is a root, $\\rho_{i+n}=C_V\\rho_i$ for all $i\\ge1$, and\n\n$$\\Phi_+=\\{\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_{nh/2}\\},\\qquad \\Phi_-=-\\Phi_+=\\{\\rho_{nh/2+1},\\dots,\\rho_{nh}\\},$$\n\nwith all $nh$ listed roots pairwise distinct; hence the sequence has exact period $nh$. Moreover $|\\Phi_+|=|T|=nh/2=\\ell(w_0)$. If $h$ is even then $w_0=c^{h/2}$. If $h$ is odd then $n$ is even, $r=|J|=|K|=n/2$, and $w_0=c^{(h-1)/2}a$. Accordingly, the word\n\n$$(s_1\\cdots s_n)^{h/2}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad(s_1\\cdots s_n)^{(h-1)/2}s_1\\cdots s_r$$\n\nis a reduced expression of $w_0$ of length $nh/2$, with prefix roots exactly $\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_{nh/2}$ in this order.\n\n**(4) Invertibility of the linear action minus the identity.** The operator $C_V-\\mathrm{id}_V$ is invertible. Consequently the conditional map from the preceding definition is defined on all of $V$ and satisfies $\\mu(\\rho_i)=\\mu_i$ for every $i\\ge1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-cg-ordered-root-pairings-and-simple-systems",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) The basic identities.** For every $i\\ge1$: $(c-\\mathrm{id})\\mu_i=-2\\rho_i$, $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_i=1$, and $\\mu_i\\in F(R(\\rho_i)c)$, i.e. $R(\\rho_i)c\\,\\mu_i=\\mu_i$; moreover $F(R(\\rho_i)c)$ is one dimensional and $\\mu_i$ is its unique vector satisfying $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_i=1$.\n\n**(2) Sign and vanishing identities.** (a) $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_j=-\\mu_{j+n}\\cdot\\rho_i$ for all $i,j\\ge1$; (b) $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_j\\ge0$ whenever $1\\le i\\le j\\le nh/2$; (c) $\\mu_{i+t}\\cdot\\rho_i=0$ for $1\\le t\\le n-1$ and all $i\\ge1$; (d) $\\mu_j\\cdot\\rho_i\\le0$ whenever $1\\le i<j\\le nh/2$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(1) Reflection length.** For $w\\in W$ put\n\n$$\\ell_T(w):=\\min\\{k\\in\\mathbb N:\\ \\text{there are }t_1,\\dots,t_k\\in T\\text{ with }w=t_1t_2\\cdots t_k\\},$$\n\nwhere the empty product ($k=0$) is the identity. The minimum exists because $S\\subseteq T$ and $S$ generates $W$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), so the admitted $k$ form a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb N$, which has a least element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]).",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-cg-ordered-root-complex-is-geometric-simplicial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Factorization criterion.** For any strictly increasing tuple $a_1<a_2<\\dots<a_k$ of roots in $\\Phi_+$, including the empty tuple when $k=0$,\n$$\\ell_T\\bigl(R(a_1)R(a_2)\\cdots R(a_k)c\\bigr)=n-k\\iff \\mu(a_i)\\cdot a_j=0\\quad\\text{for every }i>j.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Identify the normalized A3 model, its reflection matrices and the order-four Coxeter element.",
