        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-heine-borel-pseudocompactness-and-extreme-values",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$ be nonempty. The following are equivalent.\n\n1. $A$ is compact.\n2. $A$ is closed and bounded.\n3. $A$ is pseudocompact.\n4. Every continuous $f:A\\to\\mathbb{R}$ attains a maximum and a minimum on $A$.\n\nThis theorem is a ZF statement. The nonemptiness hypothesis is necessary for condition 4, because the empty image has neither a maximum nor a minimum.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-cg-diagram-products-and-invariant-form-comparison",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set with Coxeter matrix $m$, presented group $W$ and length\nfunction $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), with diagram\n$\\Gamma$ ([[def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type]]), and let\n$V=\\mathbb R^S$ carry the Coxeter form $B$ with canonical reflection\nhomomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$\n([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]],\n[[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]). Suppose $\\Gamma$ is disconnected\nwith connected components on the nonempty pairwise disjoint subsets\n$S_1,\\dots,S_k$ of $S$; put $W_i:=W_{S_i}$ and\n$V_i:=\\mathrm{span}\\{e_s:s\\in S_i\\}$.\n\n**(1) Direct product and length.** The subgroups $W_i$ commute elementwise,\n$W_i\\cap W_j=\\{1\\}$ for $i\\ne j$, and the multiplication map\n$\\mu:W_1\\times\\cdots\\times W_k\\to W$, $\\mu(w_1,\\dots,w_k)=w_1\\cdots w_k$, is an\nisomorphism of groups ([[def-group-isomorphism-and-automorphism]],\n[[def-external-direct-product-of-groups]]). Moreover\n$\\ell(w_1\\cdots w_k)=\\ell(w_1)+\\cdots+\\ell(w_k)$ for all $w_i\\in W_i$, the\nlengths on the right being those of the factors, which agree with the\nrestriction of $\\ell$\n([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (2)).\n\n**(2) Orthogonal decomposition.** $B(v_i,v_j)=0$ for all $v_i\\in V_i$,\n$v_j\\in V_j$, $i\\ne j$; hence $V=V_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus V_k$ is a $B$-orthogonal\ndirect sum, each $\\rho(W_i)$ preserves $V_i$ and fixes every $V_j$ ($j\\ne i$)\npointwise, and with $B_i:=B|_{V_i\\times V_i}$ the form is\n$B=B_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus B_k$.\n\n**(3) Invariant forms.** Let $\\beta$ be a symmetric bilinear form on $V$\ninvariant under $\\rho(W)$, i.e. $\\beta(\\rho(w)u,\\rho(w)v)=\\beta(u,v)$ for all\n$w\\in W$ and $u,v\\in V$.\n\n(i) For every $s\\in S$ one has $\\beta(e_s,\\cdot)=\\lambda_s\\,B(e_s,\\cdot)$ with\n$\\lambda_s:=\\beta(e_s,e_s)$; in particular $\\beta(e_s,e_t)=\\lambda_sB(e_s,e_t)$\nfor all $s,t\\in S$.\n\n(ii) $\\lambda_s=\\lambda_t$ whenever $s$ and $t$ lie in the same component of\n$\\Gamma$. Consequently there are $\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_k\\in\\mathbb R$ with\n$\\beta|_{V_i\\times V_i}=\\lambda_iB_i$ for every $i$, that is,\n$\\beta=\\lambda_1B_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus\\lambda_kB_k$; if $\\Gamma$ is connected\nthen $\\beta=\\lambda B$ for a single $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\n(iii) If in addition $\\beta$ is positive definite\n([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]]), then\n$\\lambda_i=\\beta(e_s,e_s)>0$ for every $i$ and every $s\\in S_i$, and $B$ is\npositive definite.\n\n**(4) Finite groups have positive definite form.** If $W$ is finite then $B$ is\npositive definite.",
          "uses": [
            "16.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set and let $m:S\\times S\\to\\{1,2,3,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ be a\nCoxeter matrix on $S$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), with\npresented group $W$, length function $\\ell$ and standard parabolics\n$W_T=\\langle s:s\\in T\\rangle$ for $T\\subseteq S$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]],\n[[def-group]]).\n\n**(1) The diagram.** The **Coxeter diagram** $\\Gamma(W,S)$ of $(W,S)$ has vertex\nset $S$, and two distinct vertices $s\\ne t$ are joined by an edge exactly when\n$m(s,t)\\ge3$; that edge carries the **label**\n$m(s,t)\\in\\{3,4,5,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. By convention the label $3$ is omitted\n(an unlabelled edge has label $3$) and no edge is drawn when $m(s,t)=2$. Thus\n$\\Gamma$ is a finite simple graph whose edges are labelled in\n$\\{3,4,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$\n([[def-graph-adjacency-incidence-neighbourhood-and-degree]]), and $m$ is\nrecovered from the pair $(S,\\Gamma)$ by $m(s,s)=1$; $m(s,t)=m(t,s)=2$ if\n$\\{s,t\\}$ is not an edge; $m(s,t)=3$ if $\\{s,t\\}$ is an unlabelled edge; and\n$m(s,t)=$ the label otherwise. In particular $m\\mapsto\\Gamma$ is injective on\nthe Coxeter matrices on $S$. For $T\\subseteq S$ the **subdiagram** $\\Gamma_T$ is\nthe induced labelled graph on $T$, i.e. the diagram of the restricted matrix\n$m|_{T\\times T}$.\n\n**(2) Graph-theoretic vocabulary.** A **cycle** of $\\Gamma$ is a cycle of the\nunderlying simple graph ([[def-graph-walk-trail-path-and-cycle]]); a **path** (or\n**chain**) is a diagram whose underlying graph is a path. $\\Gamma$ is\n**connected** when its underlying graph is connected\n([[def-connected-graph-and-connected-component]]); its **components** are the\nconnected components of the underlying graph, and their vertex sets are\nnonempty and partition $S$. For $s\\in S$ the **neighbours** of $s$ are\n$N(s):=\\{t\\in S:t\\ne s,\\ m(s,t)\\ge3\\}$ and the **degree** of $s$ is $|N(s)|$\n([[def-graph-adjacency-incidence-neighbourhood-and-degree]]).\n\n**(3) Irreducibility.** $(W,S)$ is **irreducible** when $\\Gamma$ is connected,\nand **reducible** otherwise. (For $S=\\emptyset$ the diagram is empty; the\ntrivial system is not called irreducible.)\n\n**(4) Finite type.** $(W,S)$ - and, by extension, $\\Gamma$ - is **of finite\ntype**, or **spherical**, when the group $W$ is finite. This is a property of\n$W$ itself: **no list of diagrams is part of the definition**, and no\npositivity, definiteness or nondegeneracy of any bilinear form, and no geometric\nrealization, is asserted here.\n\n**(5) Invariance and abstentions.** By the conventions of\n[[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], $m(s,t)$ is the order of $st$\nin $W$; consequently an isomorphism of Coxeter systems $(W,S)\\to(W',S')$ (a\ngroup isomorphism carrying $S$ onto $S'$) matches the two diagrams, so\nconnectedness, irreducibility and finite type depend only on the isomorphism\ntype of $(W,S)$. It is **not** asserted here that $W$ is the direct product of\nthe standard parabolics of its components - that is the content of\n[[lem-cg-diagram-products-and-invariant-form-comparison]] - nor that a diagram\nof finite type is one of the diagrams classified in\n[[thm-cg-finite-coxeter-classification-including-h-and-dihedral]].",
          "uses": [
            "16.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $\\|x\\|_0^2:=\\sum_{s\\in S}x_s^2$ for the standard coordinate norm and set $q(x):=x^{\\mathsf T}Ax$. If $m(s,t)=\\infty$ for distinct $s,t$, then $B(e_s+e_t,e_s+e_t)=0$, contradicting positive definiteness; hence every off-diagonal Coxeter exponent is finite. Therefore $A_{ss}=0$ and $A_{st}=-2B(e_s,e_t)=2\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))\\ge0$ for $s\\ne t$, with $A_{st}>0$ exactly on the edges of the connected diagram. Also $q(x)=2\\|x\\|_0^2-2B(x,x)<2\\|x\\|_0^2$ for every nonzero $x$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For $1\\le i\\le n$, the first $n$ prefix vectors satisfy $\\rho_i=\\alpha_i$ for $i\\le r$ and $\\rho_i=\\rho(a)\\alpha_i=\\alpha_i+\\sum_{s\\in J}A_{si}\\alpha_s$ for $i>r$: the earlier reflections in the same color class fix $\\alpha_i$, while each reflection in the other class adds its $A_{si}\\alpha_s$ and fixes the other roots of that class. Hence the coordinate matrix of $(\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_n)$ relative to the ordered basis $(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_n)$ is block upper triangular with identity diagonal blocks, so these vectors form a basis. The cyclic definitions give $\\rho_{i+n}=C_V\\rho_i$ and $\\mu_{i+n}=C_V\\mu_i$ for every $i\\ge1$, because each block of $n$ reflections has product $c$. Each $\\rho_i$ is a root, being the image of a simple root by an element of $W$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The unit sphere for $\\|\\cdot\\|_0$ is compact, so $q$ attains a maximum $\\lambda$ there; an edge $\\{s,t\\}$ gives $q(e_s+e_t)=4\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))>0$, so $\\lambda>0$, and the strict inequality of 1.1 at a maximizer gives $\\lambda<2$. Let $p$ be a unit maximizer. For $y$ with $\\langle p,y\\rangle_0=0$, the curve $\\gamma(t)=(p+ty)/(1+t^2\\|y\\|_0^2)^{1/2}$ stays on the unit sphere, and the derivative at $t=0$ of $q(\\gamma(t))$ is $2\\langle Ap,y\\rangle_0$; maximality makes this zero for every such $y$, so $Ap$ is a multiple of $p$, and pairing with $p$ shows $Ap=\\lambda p$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For $1\\le i\\le n$, duality gives $B(\\beta_i,\\alpha_j)=0$ for every $j\\ne i$, so $R_j\\beta_i=\\beta_i$ for every $j\\ne i$ and in particular $\\mu_i=R_1\\cdots R_{i-1}\\beta_i=\\beta_i$. Also $R_i\\beta_i=\\beta_i-2\\alpha_i$. Thus $C_V\\beta_i=R_1\\cdots R_n\\beta_i=R_1\\cdots R_{i-1}(\\beta_i-2\\alpha_i)=\\mu_i-2\\rho_i=\\beta_i-2\\rho_i$, so $(C_V-\\mathrm{id}_V)\\beta_i=-2\\rho_i$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Since every entry of $A$ is nonnegative, $q(|p|)\\ge q(p)=\\lambda$, while $\\||p|\\|_0=1$, so $|p|$ is also a maximizer and satisfies $A|p|=\\lambda|p|$ by the stationary-point argument in 2.1. Put $r:=|p|$. If $r_s>0$ and $A_{st}>0$, then $\\lambda r_t=\\sum_uA_{tu}r_u\\ge A_{ts}r_s>0$; since $\\lambda>0$ and the edge graph of $A$ is connected, positivity propagates to every coordinate. Thus we may take $p\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}^S$, proving the existence claim in (1).",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "The vectors $\\beta_1,\\dots,\\beta_n$ form a basis by the construction in the preceding definition, and $\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_n$ form a basis by 1.2; thus 2.2 shows that $C_V-\\mathrm{id}_V$ maps one basis to the other up to the nonzero scalar $-2$, so it is invertible. If $i=mn+j$ with $m\\ge0$ and $1\\le j\\le n$, then the cyclic recursions in F1 and the identity in 2.2 give $(C_V-\\mathrm{id}_V)\\mu_i=C_V^m(C_V-\\mathrm{id}_V)\\mu_j=-2C_V^m\\rho_j=-2\\rho_i$. Applying the conditional map of the preceding definition now yields $\\mu(\\rho_i)=\\mu_i$ for all $i\\ge1$, proving (4) and the definition's well-definedness justification.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Put $d':=\\sum_{t\\in K}p_t^2$. Since distinct simple roots in either color class are orthogonal, $B(u,u)=d$ and $B(v,v)=d'$. Multiplying the eigenvector equations $\\sum_{t\\in K}A_{st}p_t=\\lambda p_s$ by $p_s$ and summing over $s\\in J$ gives $\\sum_{s\\in J,t\\in K}p_sA_{st}p_t=\\lambda d$; summing the equations indexed by $K$ gives the same left side equal to $\\lambda d'$, hence $d=d'>0$. Finally $B(u,v)=-\\frac12\\sum_{s\\in J,t\\in K}p_sA_{st}p_t=-\\lambda d/2$. The Gram matrix on $(u,v)$ is $d\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}1&-\\lambda/2\\\\-\\lambda/2&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$ with determinant $d^2(1-\\lambda^2/4)>0$, so $u,v$ are independent and $P$ is a plane.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For $s\\in J$, $R_s$ fixes $\\alpha_{s'}$ for $s'\\in J\\setminus\\{s\\}$ and negates $\\alpha_s$; the corresponding statement holds in $K$. For $t\\in K$, the commuting reflections in $a$ give $\\rho(a)\\alpha_t=\\alpha_t+\\sum_{s\\in J}A_{st}\\alpha_s$, and for $s\\in J$, $\\rho(b)\\alpha_s=\\alpha_s+\\sum_{t\\in K}A_{ts}\\alpha_t$. Summing with coefficients $p_t$ and $p_s$ and using $Ap=\\lambda p$ yields $\\rho(a)u=-u$, $\\rho(a)v=v+\\lambda u$, $\\rho(b)v=-v$, and $\\rho(b)u=u+\\lambda v$. Thus $P$ is invariant under $\\rho(a),\\rho(b)$ and $C_V=\\rho(a)\\rho(b)$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "In the basis $(u,v)$, $\\rho(a)=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}-1&\\lambda\\\\0&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, $\\rho(b)=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}1&0\\\\\\lambda&-1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, and $C_V|_P=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}\\lambda^2-1&-\\lambda\\\\\\lambda&-1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$. The vectors $f_1:=u/\\sqrt d$ and $f_2:=(v+\\frac\\lambda2u)/\\sqrt{d(1-\\lambda^2/4)}$ are $B$-orthonormal, and direct substitution gives $C_Vf_1=(\\lambda^2/2-1)f_1+\\lambda\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^2/4}\\,f_2$. With $\\theta:=\\arccos(\\lambda/2)\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ these coefficients are $\\cos(2\\theta)$ and $\\sin(2\\theta)$; since $C_V$ preserves $B$ and has determinant $1$ on $P$, it is the rotation through $2\\theta$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "For $x=\\xi u+\\eta v\\in P$, one has $B(x,\\alpha_s)=p_s(\\xi-\\frac\\lambda2\\eta)$ for $s\\in J$ and $B(x,\\alpha_t)=p_t(\\eta-\\frac\\lambda2\\xi)$ for $t\\in K$. Thus $\\mathcal C\\cap P$ is given by $\\xi\\ge\\frac\\lambda2\\eta$ and $\\eta\\ge\\frac\\lambda2\\xi$; because $0<\\lambda/2<1$, these inequalities imply $\\xi,\\eta\\ge0$, and their strict versions place the relative interior in $\\mathcal C^\\circ$. Its boundary rays are $x_J:=\\lambda u+2v$ (the $J$ simple-root hyperplanes) and $x_K:=2u+\\lambda v$ (the $K$ simple-root hyperplanes). Their squared $B$-norms are both $d(4-\\lambda^2)$ and $B(x_J,x_K)=\\lambda d(2-\\lambda^2/2)$, so the sector angle has cosine $\\lambda/2$ and equals $\\theta$. The restrictions of $\\rho(a)$ and $\\rho(b)$ are reflections in the lines $\\mathbb R x_J$ and $\\mathbb R x_K$: they fix those lines, are involutions, and are nonidentity on $P$ by 4.1.",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-8-1",
          "claim": "Let $m_0$ be the order of $C_V|_P$. Since it rotates through $2\\theta\\in(0,\\pi)$, $m_0\\ge3$ and $2\\theta=2\\pi j/m_0$ for an integer $j$ with $1\\le j<m_0/2$ and $\\gcd(j,m_0)=1$. The subgroup $D:=\\langle\\rho(a)|_P,\\rho(b)|_P\\rangle$ consists of the $m_0$ distinct rotations $(C_V|_P)^k$ and the $m_0$ distinct reflections $\\rho(a)|_P(C_V|_P)^k$, so $|D|=2m_0$. Distinct elements of $D$ have lifts $\\tilde g\\in\\langle a,b\\rangle$ with distinct restrictions to $P$, hence are distinct elements of $W$; by F4 their chamber interiors are distinct and disjoint. Thus the orbit of $S_0:=\\mathcal C\\cap P$ consists of $2m_0$ distinct sectors of angle $\\theta$, each of the form $\\rho(\\tilde g)\\mathcal C\\cap P$, with pairwise disjoint relative interiors. Hence $2m_0\\theta\\le2\\pi$, so $j=1$ and $\\theta=\\pi/m_0$; equality shows the sectors cover $P$. As $m_0\\mid h$ by F1, the element $c^{m_0}$ fixes every point of $P$, in particular a point of $\\mathcal C^\\circ\\cap P$; its stabiliser is trivial, so $c^{m_0}=1$ and $h\\mid m_0$. Thus $m_0=h$ and $\\lambda=2\\cos(\\pi/h)$, completing (1) and the angle claims of (2).",
          "step": "8.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 6.1",
            "step 7.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-9-1",
          "claim": "For every root $\\alpha$, the trace $H_\\alpha\\cap P$ is a line: if $P\\subseteq H_\\alpha$, the nonidentity reflection $t_\\alpha$ fixes a point of $\\mathcal C^\\circ\\cap P$, contradicting its trivial stabiliser; otherwise the nonzero linear functional $x\\mapsto B(x,\\alpha)$ restricts to a nonzero functional on the two-plane $P$, whose kernel is a line. Each such line is a root-hyperplane trace and cannot meet the relative interior of any sector $\\rho(\\tilde g)S_0$, because that relative interior lies in the chamber interior $\\rho(\\tilde g)\\mathcal C^\\circ$, which is disjoint from the arrangement. Conversely the two boundary lines of $S_0$ are traces of simple-root hyperplanes by 7.1, and their images under $D$ are root-hyperplane traces because $\\rho(W)$ permutes roots. These images are all the sector boundary lines: the $2h$ sectors from 8.1 tile $P$ and have $2h$ distinct boundary rays, while every root trace must be one of those lines because it cannot meet a sector interior. Opposite rays define the same line, so the traces are exactly $h$ distinct lines. The connected components of the complement of these lines are the sector interiors. Each component is connected and the ambient chamber interiors form a disjoint open cover of it, so it lies in one chamber interior. Conversely, if a full-dimensional chamber section contained points from two components, choose them in its relative interior; their segment lies in that relative interior by convexity and crosses a root trace, contradicting disjointness of chamber interiors from the arrangement. Hence the full-dimensional chamber sections are exactly the $2h$ sectors.",
          "step": "9.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 7.1",
            "step 8.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-10-1",
          "claim": "The ray $\\mathbb R_{>0}x_J$ lies in the relative interior of the face $\\mathcal C_J$, so its stabiliser is $W_J$; similarly the stabiliser of $\\mathbb R_{>0}x_K$ is $W_K$. Every element of $W_J$ reduces to a product of a subset $I\\subseteq J$ because its simple generators are commuting involutions; the subset products are distinct because their $\\rho$-images have different signs on the independent simple roots and $\\rho$ is faithful by [F2]. The image of the product for $I$ acts by $-1$ on $\\operatorname{span}\\{\\alpha_s:s\\in I\\}$ and by $+1$ on its $B$-orthogonal complement, so its $(-1)$-eigenspace has dimension $|I|$; therefore it is a reflection exactly when $|I|=1$, and the reflections in $W_J$ are precisely its $|J|=r$ simple generators. The same reasoning gives exactly $|K|=n-r$ reflections in $W_K$. The $2h$ distinct boundary rays from 8.1 alternate between the two types, so there are $h$ of each; conjugating the stabiliser count around them gives $hr+h(n-r)=hn$ incidences between rays and ambient root hyperplanes. Each hyperplane contributes exactly two incidences because its trace is a line. Hence the arrangement has $hn/2$ hyperplanes, so $|T|=hn/2$ by the root-reflection correspondence of [F7].",
          "step": "10.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "step 6.1",
            "step 7.1",
            "step 8.1",
            "step 9.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-11-1",
          "claim": "If $h$ is odd, a semicircle from a point of $\\mathcal C^\\circ\\cap P$ to its antipode crosses each trace line once; since every root-hyperplane trace is one of these lines, it meets each ambient root hyperplane exactly once. The boundary-ray types alternate, so one type occurs $(h+1)/2$ times and the other $(h-1)/2$ times. Counting the hyperplanes met using 10.1 gives $hn/2=((h+1)/2)r+((h-1)/2)(n-r)$ if the more frequent rays have type $J$, and the same equation with $r$ and $n-r$ interchanged if they have type $K$. Either equation gives $r=n/2$. Thus $n$ is even and $|J|=|K|=n/2$.",
          "step": "11.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 9.1",
            "step 10.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-12-1",
          "claim": "Choose the direction around the circle so that the first boundary ray of $S_0$ is $x_J$. The rays then occur in the cyclic order $p_{2k}=C_V^k\\mathbb R_{>0}x_J$ and $p_{2k+1}=C_V^k\\rho(a)\\mathbb R_{>0}x_K$ for $k=0,\\dots,h-1$: $\\rho(a)$ reflects across the $J$ boundary, while $C_V$ advances by two sector angles. The hyperplanes through $p_{2k}$ are exactly $H_{C_V^k\\alpha_s}$ for $s=s_1,\\dots,s_r$, and those through $p_{2k+1}$ are exactly $H_{C_V^k\\rho(a)\\alpha_t}$ for $t=s_{r+1},\\dots,s_n$, by the stabiliser counts and the description of the reflections in $W_J,W_K$ from 10.1. These are precisely the blocks $H_{\\rho_{kn+1}},\\dots,H_{\\rho_{kn+r}}$ and $H_{\\rho_{kn+r+1}},\\dots,H_{\\rho_{(k+1)n}}$ by the cyclic prefix definition in 1.2. The first $h$ rays carry $hn/2$ hyperplanes: this is $hn/2$ from $h/2$ complete color pairs when $h$ is even, and $n(h-1)/2+r=hn/2$ when $h$ is odd by 11.1. The opposite semicircle carries the other $hn/2$ hyperplanes. Each half-turn list is pairwise distinct because every trace line meets that semicircle only once, and the listed hyperplanes in each boundary block are distinct simple-root images.",
          "step": "12.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 9.1",
            "step 10.1",
            "step 11.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-13-1",
          "claim": "Fix $k\\le hn/2$. The first half-turn list of hyperplanes in 12.1 is pairwise distinct, so $\\rho_k\\ne\\pm\\rho_j$ for every $j<k$. For $1\\le j\\le k$, put $y_j:=R_{j-1}\\cdots R_1\\rho_k$, with $y_1=\\rho_k$ and $y_k=\\alpha_k$; for $j<k$, $y_j\\ne\\pm\\alpha_j$, since otherwise applying $R_1\\cdots R_{j-1}$ would give $\\rho_k=\\pm\\rho_j$. Each $R_j$ preserves the sign of every root other than $\\pm\\alpha_j$ by [F3], so $y_j$ and $y_{j+1}=R_jy_j$ have the same sign. Since $y_k=\\alpha_k\\in\\Phi_+$, descending induction gives $\\rho_k\\in\\Phi_+$ for every $k\\le hn/2$.",
          "step": "13.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 12.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-14-1",
          "claim": "Let $q:=hn/2$. The word $w=c^{h/2}$ for even $h$, or $w=c^{(h-1)/2}a$ for odd $h$, has exactly $q$ letters; in the odd case this uses $r=n/2$ from 11.1. Its prefix roots are $\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_q$ by the cyclic definition, and these are positive by 13.1. At each step the root-length criterion therefore increases the prefix length by one, so the word is reduced and $\\ell(w)=q=\\ell(w_0)$. By uniqueness of the longest element, $w=w_0$, proving the formulas for $w_0$ in (3).",
          "step": "14.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 11.1",
            "step 13.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-15-1",
          "claim": "The full word $(s_1\\cdots s_n)^h$ represents $1$ and splits after its first $q$ letters, whose product is $w_0$ by 14.1. Its remaining $q$-letter suffix also represents $w_0^{-1}=w_0$, so it is reduced. Every prefix of a reduced word is reduced; applying the root-length criterion at each next letter shows that every prefix root of this suffix is positive. Its corresponding global prefix root is obtained by applying $\\rho(w_0)$ and is therefore negative by [F6]. The second half in 12.1 has pairwise distinct hyperplanes, so these negative roots are pairwise distinct as well. Hence the first $q$ roots are all of $\\Phi_+$ because $|\\Phi_+|=|T|=q$ by [F6] and 10.1, and the second $q$ are all of $\\Phi_-=-\\Phi_+$. The first and second lists are disjoint by sign, giving pairwise distinctness of all $nh$ roots; $C_V^h=\\mathrm{id}_V$ gives period $nh$, and that distinctness makes it exact. This proves (3).",
          "step": "15.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 10.1",
            "step 12.1",
            "step 13.1",
            "step 14.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-16-1",
          "claim": "In rank one, $B(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_1)=1$, $C_V=-\\mathrm{id}$, $\\rho_1=\\alpha_1$, $\\rho_2=-\\alpha_1$, and $\\mu=\\mathrm{id}_V$, so the stated conclusions hold. For a reducible finite-type system, $V$, $B$, $\\rho$, the root systems and $C_V$ split over the components by [F9]; each rank-one or irreducible component satisfies the result just proved, so $C_V-\\mathrm{id}_V$ is a direct sum of invertible operators and the map $\\mu$ and root enumeration hold componentwise. The total number of positive roots is $\\sum_i n_i h_i/2$, with no common $nh/2$ formula asserted when the component orders differ. No Choice is used.",
          "step": "16.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F9",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No empty root list is asserted: rank n>=2 has simple roots, and the separate rank-one case has the positive root alpha_1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
