        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-cg-orthogonal-wall-form-and-subspace-restriction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(V,B)$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]); on this page $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with the positive definite Coxeter form $B$ of a Coxeter system of finite type ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), and $\\mathrm O(V)$ is the group of $B$-preserving invertible linear maps ([[def-linear-isometry-and-orthogonal-or-unitary-operator]]). Let $M$, $F$ and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$ be as in [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]]. Then:\n\n**(1) Basic identities.** For every $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ one has $F(A)=F(A^{-1})$, $M(A)=M(A^{-1})$, $M(A)=F(A)^\\perp$, $V=M(A)\\oplus F(A)$, $A(M(A))=M(A)$, $A(F(A))=F(A)$, and $(A-\\mathrm{id})|_{M(A)}:M(A)\\to M(A)$ is a bijection ([[cor-double-orthogonal-complement-and-dimension]], [[thm-rank-nullity]]). For all $X,Y\\in\\mathrm O(V)$, $M(XY)\\subseteq M(X)+X\\,M(Y)$ and hence $\\dim M(XY)\\le\\dim M(X)+\\dim M(Y)$.\n\n**(2) The Wall form.** For $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ put\n\n$$\\chi_A(u,v):=B\\bigl((A-\\mathrm{id})|_{M(A)}^{-1}u,\\ v\\bigr)\\qquad(u,v\\in M(A)).$$\n\nThen $\\chi_A$ is a bilinear form on $M(A)$ satisfying\n\n$$\\chi_A(u,v)+\\chi_A(v,u)=-B(u,v)\\qquad(u,v\\in M(A));$$\n\nin particular $\\chi_A$ is nondegenerate and its symmetric part is $-\\tfrac12B|_{M(A)}$ ([[def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces]], [[prop-adjoint-algebra]]).\n\n**(3) Subspace restriction.** Let $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$, let $U\\subseteq M(A)$ be a subspace and let $\\Pi_U:V\\to U$ be the orthogonal projection ([[def-orthogonal-projection]], [[thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition]]). There is a unique $H_U\\in\\operatorname{End}(U)$ with $B(H_Uu,v)=\\chi_A(u,v)$ for all $u,v\\in U$, and it satisfies $H_U+H_U^*=-\\mathrm{id}_U$, so that $H_U$ is invertible. Define $A_U\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ by $A_Uu=u+H_U^{-1}u$ for $u\\in U$ and $A_U=\\mathrm{id}$ on $U^\\perp$. Then $M(A_U)=U$ and\n\n$$(A_U-\\mathrm{id})|_U^{-1}=H_U,\\qquad H_Uu=\\Pi_U\\bigl((A-\\mathrm{id})|_{M(A)}\\bigr)^{-1}u\\ \\ (u\\in U).$$\n\nAn element $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ is a reflection (that is, $\\dim M(A)=1$) if and only if $A=\\mathrm{id}-2\\Pi_L$ for the line $L=M(A)$; in particular every line $L\\subseteq V$ is the moved space of exactly one reflection of $\\mathrm O(V)$ ([[thm-finite-dimensional-isometry-characterisations]]).\n\n**(4) The restriction theorem.** For every subspace $U\\subseteq M(A)$ one has $A_U\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$, that is $\\dim M(A)=\\dim U+\\dim M(A_U^{-1}A)$; and for $U\\subseteq U'\\subseteq M(A)$ one has $(A_{U'})_U=A_U$, hence $A_U\\le_{\\mathrm O}A_{U'}$. Conversely every $B\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ with $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ satisfies $M(B)\\subseteq M(A)$, $\\chi_A|_{M(B)}=\\chi_B$ and $B=A_{M(B)}$. Consequently the assignment $U\\mapsto A_U$ is a bijection from the set of subspaces of $M(A)$ onto $\\{B\\in\\mathrm O(V):B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A\\}$, with inverse $B\\mapsto M(B)$, and it is an order isomorphism for inclusion of subspaces and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$.\n\n**(5) Rank-length equality and prefixes.** Let $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ and let $r_1,\\dots,r_k\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ be reflections with $A=r_1r_2\\cdots r_k$. Then $k\\ge\\dim M(A)$, and $A$ is a product of exactly $\\dim M(A)$ reflections. Moreover $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ if and only if there are a shortest factorization $A=r_1\\cdots r_m$ (so $m=\\dim M(A)$) and an index $k$ with $B=r_1\\cdots r_k$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-cg-carter-reflection-length-and-absolute-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, with $V=\\mathbb R^S$, positive definite Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection representation $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi$, reflection set $T$ and length function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), and let $\\ell_T$, $M$, $F$ and $\\le_T$ be as in [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]]. Then:\n\n**(1) Carter's formula.** For every $w\\in W$,\n\n$$\\ell_T(w)=\\dim M(w)=\\dim V-\\dim F(w).$$\n\n**(2) The absolute order.** $\\le_T$ is a partial order on $W$ ([[def-partial-order]]), and:\n\n**(i)** $u\\le_T v$ holds if and only if there are reflections $t_1,\\dots,t_m\\in T$ and an index $k\\le m$ such that $u=t_1\\cdots t_k$ and $v=t_1\\cdots t_m$ are shortest reflection factorizations, that is, $k=\\ell_T(u)$ and $m=\\ell_T(v)$ (a shortest reflection factorization of $u$ is a prefix of one of $v$);\n\n**(ii)** $\\ell_T(1)=0$, $u<_T v$ implies $\\ell_T(u)<\\ell_T(v)$, and $\\ell_T(y)=\\ell_T(x)+1$ whenever $y$ covers $x$; hence $\\ell_T$ is a rank function and every interval $[u,v]_T=\\{x\\in W:u\\le_Tx\\le_Tv\\}$ is finite ([[def-graded-poset-and-rank]]);\n\n**(iii)** $|\\ell_T(u)-\\ell_T(v)|\\le\\ell_T(u^{-1}v)$, $\\ell_T(u^{-1})=\\ell_T(u)$ and $\\ell_T(vuv^{-1})=\\ell_T(u)$ for all $u,v\\in W$;\n\n**(iv)** $u\\le_T v$ implies $M(u)\\subseteq M(v)$ and $F(v)\\subseteq F(u)$.\n\n**(3) Moved-space rigidity under a common upper bound.** Let $\\alpha,\\beta,\\delta\\in W$ with $\\alpha\\le_T\\delta$ and $\\beta\\le_T\\delta$. Then\n\n$$\\alpha\\le_T\\beta\\iff M(\\alpha)\\subseteq M(\\beta);$$\n\nin particular $M(\\alpha)=M(\\beta)$ implies $\\alpha=\\beta$, and $u\\mapsto M(u)$ is an order isomorphism from $[1,\\delta]_T$ onto its image ordered by inclusion. The proof of the converse uses the common upper bound $\\delta$, through the restriction of $\\delta$ to the subspace $M(\\alpha)$; the converse is claimed only under this hypothesis (see the companion example of the $I_2(4)$ rotation, where the hypothesis fails).",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-cg-reflection-factorizations-and-independent-normals",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, with $V=\\mathbb R^S$, positive definite Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection representation $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi$ and reflection set $T$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), with the chamber system $wC$, the open faces $wC_I$ and the root hyperplanes $H_\\alpha=\\{x\\in V:B(x,\\alpha)=0\\}$ of the transferred dual action ([[def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers]]), and let $\\ell_T$, $M$, $F$ and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$ be as in [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]]; for $u\\in W$ write $M(u)=M(\\rho(u))$ and $F(u)=F(\\rho(u))$. Then:\n\n**(1) Root normals in the moved space.** Let $w\\in W$ with $w\\ne1$. Then $\\dim M(w)>0$ and there is a root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ with $F(w)\\subseteq H_\\alpha$. For every such root one has $\\alpha\\in M(w)$, and the reflection $t_\\alpha\\in T$ with $\\rho(t_\\alpha)=r_\\alpha$ ([[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1), [[lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders]] (2)) satisfies\n\n$$\\rho(t_\\alpha)\\le_{\\mathrm O}\\rho(w),\\qquad \\dim M(w)=1+\\dim M(t_\\alpha w).$$\n\n**(2) Factorizations and Carter's formula.** Every $w\\in W$ is a product $w=t_1t_2\\cdots t_k$ of $k=\\dim M(w)$ reflections $t_i\\in T$, and no product of fewer elements of $T$ represents $w$; equivalently\n\n$$\\ell_T(w)=\\dim M(w).$$\n\n**(3) Independent normals.** Let $t_1,\\dots,t_m\\in T$ and let $\\alpha_i\\in\\Phi$ be the root with $\\rho(t_i)=r_{\\alpha_i}$. Then $\\dim M(t_1\\cdots t_m)\\le m$; and if $\\dim M(t_1\\cdots t_m)=m$ — in particular if $t_1\\cdots t_m$ is a shortest reflection factorization of its product — then the $m$ vectors\n\n$$\\alpha_1,\\ \\rho(t_1)\\alpha_2,\\ \\rho(t_1t_2)\\alpha_3,\\ \\dots,\\ \\rho(t_1t_2\\cdots t_{m-1})\\alpha_m$$\n\nare linearly independent ([[def-linear-independence]]) and span $M(t_1\\cdots t_m)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-cg-reflection-factorizations-and-independent-normals",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, with $V=\\mathbb R^S$, positive definite Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection representation $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi$ and reflection set $T$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), with the chamber system $wC$, the open faces $wC_I$ and the root hyperplanes $H_\\alpha=\\{x\\in V:B(x,\\alpha)=0\\}$ of the transferred dual action ([[def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers]]), and let $\\ell_T$, $M$, $F$ and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$ be as in [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]]; for $u\\in W$ write $M(u)=M(\\rho(u))$ and $F(u)=F(\\rho(u))$. Then:\n\n**(1) Root normals in the moved space.** Let $w\\in W$ with $w\\ne1$. Then $\\dim M(w)>0$ and there is a root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ with $F(w)\\subseteq H_\\alpha$. For every such root one has $\\alpha\\in M(w)$, and the reflection $t_\\alpha\\in T$ with $\\rho(t_\\alpha)=r_\\alpha$ ([[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1), [[lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders]] (2)) satisfies\n\n$$\\rho(t_\\alpha)\\le_{\\mathrm O}\\rho(w),\\qquad \\dim M(w)=1+\\dim M(t_\\alpha w).$$\n\n**(2) Factorizations and Carter's formula.** Every $w\\in W$ is a product $w=t_1t_2\\cdots t_k$ of $k=\\dim M(w)$ reflections $t_i\\in T$, and no product of fewer elements of $T$ represents $w$; equivalently\n\n$$\\ell_T(w)=\\dim M(w).$$\n\n**(3) Independent normals.** Let $t_1,\\dots,t_m\\in T$ and let $\\alpha_i\\in\\Phi$ be the root with $\\rho(t_i)=r_{\\alpha_i}$. Then $\\dim M(t_1\\cdots t_m)\\le m$; and if $\\dim M(t_1\\cdots t_m)=m$ — in particular if $t_1\\cdots t_m$ is a shortest reflection factorization of its product — then the $m$ vectors\n\n$$\\alpha_1,\\ \\rho(t_1)\\alpha_2,\\ \\rho(t_1t_2)\\alpha_3,\\ \\dots,\\ \\rho(t_1t_2\\cdots t_{m-1})\\alpha_m$$\n\nare linearly independent ([[def-linear-independence]]) and span $M(t_1\\cdots t_m)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, $n:=\\lvert S\\rvert$, and length\nfunction $\\ell$; thus $W$ is a finite group\n([[def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type]], clause (4),\n[[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]). On $V:=\\mathbb R^S$ let $B$ be the Coxeter\nform, let $r_a$ be the reflection with normal $a$ (defined when $B(a,a)\\ne0$), and let\n$\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ be the canonical reflection homomorphism with root system\n$$\\Phi=\\{\\rho(w)e_s:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subset V$$\nand reflection set $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subset W$\n([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]).\nLet $V^*$ be the algebraic dual with its dual action, its closed chamber $C$, its interior\n$C^\\circ$ and its root hyperplanes $H_\\alpha$\n([[def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes]],\n[[lem-cg-dual-action-and-chamber-faces-exist]]).\n\nSince $W$ is finite, $B$ is positive definite\n([[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]], clause (1),\n[[lem-cg-diagram-products-and-invariant-form-comparison]], clause (4)); hence\n$b:V\\to V^*$, $b(v):=B(v,\\cdot)$, is a linear isomorphism with\n$b(\\rho(w)v)=w\\cdot b(v)$ for all $w\\in W$ and $v\\in V$, because $B$ is $\\rho$-invariant\n([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]],\n[[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]]). **Identify $V$ with $V^*$ by $b$ only\nnow**, and transfer $C$, $C^\\circ$ and the $H_\\alpha$ along $b^{-1}$; with the same letters\n\n$$C=\\{v\\in V:B(v,e_s)\\ge0\\text{ for every }s\\in S\\},\\qquad C^\\circ=\\{v\\in V:B(v,e_s)>0\\text{ for every }s\\in S\\},\\qquad H_\\alpha=\\{v\\in V:B(v,\\alpha)=0\\}.$$\n\nGive $V$ the metric topology of the inner product $B$\n([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[def-metric-space]],\n[[def-metric-topology]]); it is canonical because $B$ is determined by $(W,S)$, and $b$ is an\nisometry for $B$ and its transferred form, so topological notions may be moved across the\nidentification.\n\n**(1) The finite reflection arrangement.** Put\n$$\\mathcal A:=\\{H_\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}.$$\nEach $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ satisfies $B(\\alpha,\\alpha)=1\\ne0$\n([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]], clause (3)), so $H_\\alpha$ is\nthe kernel of the nonzero linear functional $B(\\cdot,\\alpha)$ and in particular contains $0$;\n$\\mathcal A$ is finite because $\\Phi$ is the image of the finite set $W\\times S$ under\n$(w,s)\\mapsto\\rho(w)e_s$; and $\\mathcal A$ is permuted by $\\rho(W)$, since\n$\\rho(w)H_\\alpha=H_{\\rho(w)\\alpha}$ for all $w\\in W$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ (the form $B$ is\n$\\rho$-invariant). A **chamber** of $\\mathcal A$ is a connected component of\n$V\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}H_\\alpha$, and a **closed chamber** is the closure of a\nchamber ([[def-connected-space]], [[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]],\n[[def-metric-topology]]). For $I\\subseteq S$ put\n$$\\overline{C_I}:=\\{v\\in C:B(v,e_s)=0\\text{ for }s\\in I\\},\\qquad C_I:=\\{v\\in C:B(v,e_s)=0\\text{ for }s\\in I\\text{ and }B(v,e_s)>0\\text{ for }s\\notin I\\},$$\nthe **closed face** and the **open face** of $C$ of type $I$; for $w\\in W$ put\n$wC:=\\rho(w)C$, $w\\overline{C_I}:=\\rho(w)\\overline{C_I}$ and $wC_I:=\\rho(w)C_I$.\n\n**(2) The spherical chamber complex.** The form $B$ is an inner product on $V$; let\n$\\lVert v\\rVert_B:=B(v,v)^{1/2}$ and let\n$$S^{n-1}:=\\{v\\in V:\\lVert v\\rVert_B=1\\}$$\nbe the **unit sphere**, with the subspace topology\n([[cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]],\n[[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]); the exponent is notation for the unit sphere of\nthe $n$-dimensional inner-product space $(V,B)$, and no dimension theory of spheres is claimed\nhere. The **spherical chamber** belonging to $w\\in W$ is $wC\\cap S^{n-1}$, and its **spherical\nfaces** are the sets $w\\overline{C_I}\\cap S^{n-1}$.\n\n**(3) The coset face poset.** For $I\\subseteq S$ put $W_I:=\\langle s:s\\in I\\rangle\\le W$\n([[def-generated-subgroup]]) and let $wW_I:=\\{wu:u\\in W_I\\}$ be a left coset\n([[def-coset]]). The **coset face poset** is the set\n$$\\{wW_I:w\\in W,\\ I\\subsetneq S\\}$$\nof cosets of proper standard parabolics, ordered by **reverse inclusion** as subsets of $W$:\n$\\sigma\\preceq\\tau$ if and only if $\\sigma\\supseteq\\tau$.\n\n**(4) Abstentions.** This definition asserts neither that the sets $wC^\\circ$ are the connected\ncomponents of $V\\setminus\\bigcup_\\alpha H_\\alpha$ nor that they cover it, neither that the\ncosets $wW_I$ index the faces $w\\overline{C_I}$ nor that the spherical faces triangulate\n$S^{n-1}$; those assertions are proved in\n[[thm-cg-finite-chamber-tiling-and-coset-face-identification]], the recorded justifier of this\ndefinition. Separately, the $B$-dual family of the basis $(e_s)$ is provided by\n[[def-dual-family-associated-to-a-basis]], [[def-linear-basis]] and\n[[thm-dual-family-is-a-basis-in-finite-dimension]], and no Choice is used: $S$ and $W$ are\nfinite and all objects here are finite-dimensional or set-theoretic.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-cg-finite-chamber-tiling-and-coset-face-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, $n:=|S|$, and let the\narrangement $\\mathcal A$, the chamber $C$, its interior $C^\\circ$, the closed and open faces\n$\\overline{C_I}$ and $C_I$, the translated objects $wC$, $w\\overline{C_I}$, $wC_I$, the unit\nsphere $S^{n-1}$ and the coset face poset be as in\n[[def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers]]. Let $\\rho$,\n$\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$, $V_+$, $T$, $\\ell$ be as in\n[[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]],\n[[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]] and\n[[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]; let $S(u)$ denote the **support** of $u\\in W$,\nthe set of letters occurring in a reduced expression of $u$\n([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]], clause (1)); and let\n$U=\\bigcup_{w\\in W}wC\\subseteq V^*$ be the Tits cone of the dual action with its negative-root\nsets $\\operatorname{Neg}(f)=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+:f(\\alpha)<0\\}$\n([[def-cg-tits-cone-and-fundamental-chamber]]). Then:\n\n**(1) The chamber tiling.** $U=V^*$, and under the identification of the definition\n$V=\\bigcup_{w\\in W}wC$; moreover\n$$V\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}H_\\alpha=\\bigsqcup_{w\\in W}wC^\\circ,$$\nthe connected components of $V\\setminus\\bigcup_\\alpha H_\\alpha$ are exactly the sets\n$wC^\\circ$ $(w\\in W)$ with closures $\\overline{wC^\\circ}=wC$, so the closed chambers of\n$\\mathcal A$ are exactly the sets $wC$; the map $w\\mapsto wC^\\circ$ is a bijection from $W$\nonto the set of chambers, distinct closed chambers have disjoint interiors, and every\n$W$-orbit in $V$ meets $C$ in exactly one point.\n\n**(2) Simplicial chambers and their vertices.** Let $v_s\\in V$ be the $B$-dual basis of\n$(e_s)$, i.e. $B(v_s,e_t)=\\delta_{st}$ ([[def-dual-family-associated-to-a-basis]],\n[[thm-dual-family-is-a-basis-in-finite-dimension]]). Then\n$$\\overline{C_I}=\\Bigl\\{\\sum_{s\\notin I}\\lambda_sv_s:\\lambda_s\\ge0\\Bigr\\},\\qquad C_I=\\Bigl\\{\\sum_{s\\notin I}\\lambda_sv_s:\\lambda_s>0\\Bigr\\}$$\nfor every $I\\subseteq S$; the closed chamber $C$ is the simplicial cone with extreme rays\n$\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}v_s$ $(s\\in S)$; the vertices of the spherical simplex $C\\cap S^{n-1}$ are\nexactly the points $v_s/\\lVert v_s\\rVert_B$, in the precise sense that\n$C\\cap S^{n-1}\\cap\\bigcap_{t\\ne s}H_{e_t}=\\{v_s/\\lVert v_s\\rVert_B\\}$ for every $s$; and the dihedral angle\nbetween the walls $H_{e_s}$ and $H_{e_t}$ is $\\pi/m(s,t)$ in the following exact sense: the tangent\nsector $\\{v:B(v,e_s)\\ge0,\\ B(v,e_t)\\ge0\\}$ of $C$ along the codimension-two face\n$C\\cap H_{e_s}\\cap H_{e_t}$ projects under the orthogonal projection\n$V\\to\\mathbb Re_s+\\mathbb Re_t$ onto a sector in the two-plane $\\mathbb Re_s+\\mathbb Re_t$\nbounded by its two lines $H_{e_s}$ and $H_{e_t}$, and that sector has angle $\\pi/m(s,t)$. More generally each $w\\overline{C_I}$ is a simplicial cone with the linearly\nindependent generators $\\rho(w)v_s$ $(s\\notin I)$. The walls of the chamber $wC$ are the root\nhyperplanes $wH_{e_s}=H_{\\rho(w)e_s}$.\n\n**(3) The face identification and the stabilisers.** The assignment\n$wW_I\\mapsto w\\overline{C_I}$ is a well-defined bijection from the coset face poset onto the\nset of proper faces $\\{w\\overline{C_I}:w\\in W,\\ I\\subsetneq S\\}$ (the remaining sets\n$w\\overline{C_S}$ are all equal to $\\{0\\}$, the common face of all chambers), and for all\n$w,v\\in W$ and $I,J\\subseteq S$\n$$wW_I=vW_J\\iff w\\overline{C_I}=v\\overline{C_J},\\qquad wW_I\\subseteq vW_J\\iff w\\overline{C_J}\\subseteq v\\overline{C_I},$$\n$$w\\overline{C_I}\\cap v\\overline{C_J}=w\\overline{C_{I\\cup J\\cup S(v^{-1}w)}},\\qquad wC_I\\cap vC_J\\neq\\emptyset\\iff wW_I=vW_J,$$\nso the relative interiors of the faces partition $V$; moreover\n$V\\setminus\\{0\\}=\\bigsqcup wC_I$, the disjoint union running over the cosets $wW_I$ with\n$I\\subsetneq S$. For every $x\\in wC_I$ one has\n$\\operatorname{Stab}_W(x)=wW_Iw^{-1}$, and the setwise stabiliser\n$\\{w'\\in W:w'w\\overline{C_I}=w\\overline{C_I}\\}$ of the face $w\\overline{C_I}$ is the same\nsubgroup $wW_Iw^{-1}$.\n\n**(4) The spherical triangulation.** Assume $n\\ge1$. Then\n$S^{n-1}=\\bigsqcup(wC_I\\cap S^{n-1})$, the union running over the cosets $wW_I$ with\n$I\\subsetneq S$, and\n$$\\Sigma:=\\{w\\overline{C_I}\\cap S^{n-1}:w\\in W,\\ I\\subsetneq S\\}$$\nis a finite simplicial complex in the sphere: each member is the spherical simplex whose\nvertices are the points $\\rho(w)v_s/\\lVert v_s\\rVert_B$ $(s\\notin I)$ in the same sense, the relative interiors\n$wC_I\\cap S^{n-1}$ are pairwise disjoint and cover $S^{n-1}$, and the intersection of two\nmembers is a common face by (3); its face poset is isomorphic to the coset face poset by\n$wW_I\\mapsto w\\overline{C_I}\\cap S^{n-1}$. Consequently the abstract simplicial complex $K$\nwith vertices the cosets $wW_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}$ $(w\\in W,\\ s\\in S)$ and simplices the sets\n$\\{wW_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}:s\\notin I\\}$ $(w\\in W,\\ I\\subsetneq S)$ is a triangulation of\n$S^{n-1}$: the simplexwise affine map $\\varphi:|K|\\to S^{n-1}$ that sends the barycentric\ncoordinate at the vertex $wW_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}$ to the direction of $\\rho(w)v_s$ is a\ncontinuous bijection, $|K|$ is compact because $K$ is finite\n([[prop-a-finite-simplicial-complex-has-compact-hausdorff-realization]]) and $S^{n-1}$ is\nHausdorff, so $\\varphi$ is a homeomorphism\n([[def-geometric-realization-of-an-abstract-simplicial-complex]],\n[[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]], [[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]). In\nparticular $K$ has exactly $|W|$ maximal simplices, indexed by the chambers. For $S=\\emptyset$\nthe group is trivial, $V=0$, and $S^{-1}=\\emptyset$ is triangulated by the empty complex.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$, $m$, $V=\\mathbb R^S$, $B$ and $r_a$ be as in [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]].\n\n**(1) Well-definedness of $B$.** The functions $e_s$ ($s\\in S$) form a basis of $V$, and there is exactly one symmetric bilinear form $B$ on $V$ with $B(e_s,e_t)=-\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ for finite $m(s,t)$ and $B(e_s,e_t)=-1$ for $m(s,t)=\\infty$; it satisfies $B(u,w)=\\sum_{s,t\\in S}u(s)w(t)B(e_s,e_t)$ for all $u,w\\in V$.\n\n**(2) Reflection identities.** Let $a\\in V$ with $B(a,a)\\ne0$. Then $r_a$ is linear, $r_a^2=\\mathrm{id}_V$, $r_a(a)=-a$, $r_a(v)=v$ for every $v$ with $B(v,a)=0$, and $B(r_au,r_aw)=B(u,w)$ for all $u,w\\in V$. Moreover $\\ker B(-,a)$ is a linear subspace of dimension $\\dim V-1$ (a hyperplane of $V$, in the codimension-one sense) and is fixed pointwise by $r_a$.\n\n**(3) The rank-two plane.** Let $s\\ne t$ in $S$, put $P:=\\mathbb Re_s+\\mathbb Re_t$ and $c:=c(s,t)$ as in [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]] (so $c=1$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$). Then:\n\n(i) $B|_P$ has Gram matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}1&-c\\\\-c&1\\end{pmatrix}$; for finite $m$ it is positive definite, with $B(x_se_s+x_te_t,\\,x_se_s+x_te_t)=(x_s-cx_t)^2+\\sin^2(\\pi/m)\\,x_t^2$; for $m=\\infty$ it is positive semidefinite, i.e. $B(x,x)\\ge0$ for all $x\\in P$, with radical $\\mathbb R(e_s+e_t)$.\n\n(ii) $r_s$ and $r_t$ fix every element of $P^\\perp=\\{v\\in V:B(v,e_s)=B(v,e_t)=0\\}$ pointwise; and if $m<\\infty$ then $V=P\\oplus P^\\perp$.\n\n(iii) The product $A:=r_sr_t$ has, in the ordered basis $(e_s,e_t)$ of $P$, the matrix $A=\\begin{pmatrix}4c^2-1&-2c\\\\2c&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, of determinant $1$.\n\n(iv) If $m<\\infty$ then $\\operatorname{tr}A=2\\cos(2\\pi/m)$, $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_V$, and $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_V$ for $0<k<m$. If $m=\\infty$ then $A=\\mathrm{id}_P+N$ on $P$ for some $N$ with $N\\ne0$ and $N^2=0$; hence $(r_sr_t)^k|_P=\\mathrm{id}_P+kN$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, so $r_sr_t$ has infinite order on $V$.",
          "uses": [
            "7.3",
            "9.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "lem-finite-dimensional-space-over-an-infinite-field-is-not-a-finite-union-of-proper-subspaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space over an infinite field $F$. No\nfinite family of proper linear subspaces of $V$ has union $V$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group with length $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$ and reflection set $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]]); every root has $B$-norm one.\n\n**(1) The root-reflection dictionary.** For $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ choose $w\\in W$, $s\\in S$ with $\\alpha=\\rho(w)e_s$ and put $t_\\alpha:=wsw^{-1}\\in T$. Then:\n(i) $t_\\alpha$ is independent of the chosen representation of $\\alpha$;\n(ii) $\\rho(t_\\alpha)=r_\\alpha$, the reflection with normal $\\alpha$; and $t_{\\rho(w)\\alpha}=w\\,t_\\alpha\\,w^{-1}$ for all $w\\in W$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$;\n(iii) $t_{-\\alpha}=t_\\alpha$, and for $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\Phi$ one has $t_\\alpha=t_\\beta$ if and only if $\\alpha=\\pm\\beta$;\n(iv) the induced map $\\{\\pm\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}\\to T$ is a bijection; so is the map $\\Phi_+\\to T$, $\\alpha\\mapsto t_\\alpha$.\n\n**(2) Inversion formula.** For every $w\\in W$ one has $|N(w)|=\\ell(w)$ (with $N$ as in [[def-cg-geometric-inversion-set]]); and for every reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_n$,\n$$N(w)=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_n)^{-1}e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},\\qquad N(w^{-1})=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_1\\cdots s_{i-1})e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},$$\nthe displayed elements being pairwise distinct positive roots.\n\n**(3) Strong exchange.** Let $w\\in W$ and $t\\in T$ satisfy $\\ell(tw)<\\ell(w)$, and let $w=s_1\\cdots s_n$ be a reduced expression. Then there is a unique $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ with\n$$tw=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_n,\\qquad t=r_i:=s_1\\cdots s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}\\cdots s_1;$$\nmoreover, if $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$ is the positive root with $t=t_\\alpha$, then $\\alpha\\in N(w^{-1})$ and $\\alpha=\\rho(s_1\\cdots s_{i-1})e_{s_i}$.",
          "uses": [
            "7.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For every $i\\ge1$, [F2] gives $(C-\\mathrm{id})\\mu_i=-2\\rho_i$. Since $C$ is an isometry and $B(\\rho_i,\\rho_i)=1$, $B(\\mu_i,\\rho_i)=1$ follows by expanding $B(C\\mu_i,C\\mu_i)=B(\\mu_i-2\\rho_i,\\mu_i-2\\rho_i)$. The same identity gives $C\\mu_i=\\mu_i-2\\rho_i$, so $r_{\\rho_i}C\\mu_i=\\mu_i$ and $\\mu_i\\in F(r_{\\rho_i}C)$. For $1\\le i\\le n$, the reflection word $r_{\\rho_i}C$ cancels to $R_1\\cdots R_{i-1}R_{i+1}\\cdots R_n$, so its reflection length is at most $n-1$; the triangle inequality and $\\ell_T(c)=n$ give the lower bound $n-1$. Thus its fixed space is one-dimensional. Since $r_{\\rho_{i+n}}C=C(r_{\\rho_i}C)C^{-1}$, the same dimension holds for all $i$. The vector $\\mu_i$ is nonzero because $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_i=1$, and it is the unique vector in that line with this pairing. This proves (1).",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For all $i,j\\ge1$, $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_j=-\\mu_{j+n}\\cdot\\rho_i$. Indeed, $\\rho_i=\\tfrac12(\\mu_i-C\\mu_i)$ by [F2], so\n$-\\mu_{j+n}\\cdot\\rho_i=-C\\mu_j\\cdot\\tfrac12(\\mu_i-C\\mu_i)=\\tfrac12(\\mu_j-C\\mu_j)\\cdot\\mu_i=\\rho_j\\cdot\\mu_i$, using the isometry of $C$ and [F1]-[F2]. This is (2)(a).",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Suppose $1\\le i\\le j\\le nh/2$. If $i>n$, then $j>n$ and $C$-equivariance gives $\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_j=\\mu_{i-n}\\cdot\\rho_{j-n}$. Repeating reduces to a first index at most $n$ while keeping both indices positive and ordered. For $i\\le n$, $\\mu_i=\\beta_i$, and $\\beta_i\\cdot\\rho_j$ is the coefficient of $\\alpha_i$ in the positive root $\\rho_j$; this is nonnegative by [F4]. Thus (2)(b) holds.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "Every reflection lies below $c$. Indeed, $M(c)=V$ by [F1]. For a positive root $\\alpha$, the orthogonal reflection with moved line $\\mathbb R\\alpha$ is uniquely $r_\\alpha=\\rho(t_\\alpha)$ by [F3],[F6]; the Wall restriction for this line is below $C=\\rho(c)$ and has moved space $\\mathbb R\\alpha$. The Wall dimension identity and Carter's formula therefore give $t_\\alpha\\le_Tc$.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-5",
          "claim": "We use two elementary consequences of absolute order. First, if $w=r_1\\cdots r_m$ is a reduced reflection factorization and $p<q$, then $r_pr_q\\le_Tw$: distinct root-reflections have product moved space the two-plane spanned by their distinct normal lines, so their product has reflection length $2$ by Carter's formula. Write $w=xr_pyr_qz$; then $(r_pr_q)^{-1}w=r_qr_pxr_pyr_qz=(r_q(r_pxr_p)y r_q)z$, a product of $m-2$ reflections because conjugation preserves reflections. The triangle inequality forces this complement to have length $m-2$. Second, if $u\\le_Tv\\le_Tw$, then $v^{-1}w\\le_Tu^{-1}w$: write $v=ux$, $w=vy$ with additive reflection lengths; then $u^{-1}w=xy$, while $y^{-1}xy$ has the same length as $x$, so $y\\le_Txy$. These facts will be used below.",
          "step": "1.5",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "To prove (2)(c), first reduce $i$ modulo $n$ using $C$-equivariance, which preserves $\\mu_{i+t}\\cdot\\rho_i$, and assume $1\\le i\\le n$. If $i+t\\le n$, then $\\mu_{i+t}=\\beta_{i+t}$ and $\\rho_i=R_1\\cdots R_{i-1}\\alpha_i$ has no $\\alpha_{i+t}$ coefficient, so the pairing is zero. If $i+t>n$, put $j=i+t-n$, so $1\\le j<i$. By (2)(a), $\\mu_{i+t}\\cdot\\rho_i=\\mu_{j+n}\\cdot\\rho_i=-\\mu_i\\cdot\\rho_j=-\\beta_i\\cdot\\rho_j=0$, since $\\rho_j$ is supported on $\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_j$. Now if $1\\le i<j\\le nh/2$ and $j<i+n$, (2)(d) is (2)(c) with $t=j-i$; if $j\\ge i+n$, (2)(a) and (2)(b) give $\\mu_j\\cdot\\rho_i=-\\mu_{i+n}\\cdot\\rho_j\\le0$. This proves (2)(c)-(d).",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For every $\\sigma\\le_Tc$, one has $P_\\sigma=\\Phi_+\\cap M(\\sigma)$. If $\\alpha\\in P_\\sigma$, then $M(t_\\alpha)=\\mathbb R\\alpha\\subseteq M(\\sigma)$ by [F5]. Conversely, if $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+\\cap M(\\sigma)$, then $t_\\alpha\\le_Tc$ by step 1.4 and $\\sigma\\le_Tc$, so moved-space rigidity in [F5] gives $t_\\alpha\\le_T\\sigma$. Also $P_\\sigma$ spans $M(\\sigma)$: choose a reduced reflection factorization of $\\sigma$; its prefix-conjugated normals are a basis of $M(\\sigma)$ by [F7], and the reflections with those normals have moved lines in $M(\\sigma)$, hence lie below $\\sigma$ by the same common-upper-bound argument. Taking the positive sign of each normal puts a spanning set in $P_\\sigma$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "For any increasing tuple $a_1<\\cdots<a_m$ of positive roots, one has $\\ell_T(R(a_1)\\cdots R(a_m)c)=n-m$ if and only if $\\mu(a_i)\\cdot a_j=0$ for all $i>j$. If $m=0$, the left side is $\\ell_T(c)=n$ by [F1], and the zero-pairing condition is vacuous; hence assume $m\\ge1$. Put $r_i=R(a_i)$. The length equality is equivalent to the reverse product $q=r_m\\cdots r_1$ being a reduced element below $c$: its complement is $q^{-1}c=r_1\\cdots r_m c$. In fact, if $\\ell_T(q^{-1}c)=n-m$, then $\\ell_T(q)\\le m$ and $n=\\ell_T(c)\\le\\ell_T(q)+\\ell_T(q^{-1}c)\\le n$, so both inequalities are equalities; the converse is the defining absolute-order equality. For the forward implication, the pair-product fact in step 1.5 gives $r_i r_j\\le_Tc$ whenever $i>j$. Since $\\ell_T(r_i c)=n-1$, this gives $r_j\\le_Tr_i c$ from $\\ell_T(r_j r_i c)=n-2$; hence $M(r_j)\\subseteq M(r_i c)=F(r_i c)^\\perp$. The vector $\\mu(a_i)$ is a nonzero member of the one-dimensional fixed space $F(r_i c)$, so $\\mu(a_i)\\cdot a_j=0$. Conversely, suppose all these pairings vanish. The matrix $[\\mu(a_i)\\cdot a_j]$ is upper triangular with diagonal $1$ by (1), so both the $a_i$ and the $\\mu(a_i)$ are linearly independent; in particular $m\\le n$. Induct downward on $i$ to prove $q_i:=r_m\\cdots r_i\\le_Tc$ with length $m-i+1$. The base $q_m=r_m$ is step 1.4. If $q_{i+1}\\le_Tc$, then each factor $r_j$ with $j>i$ satisfies $r_j\\le_Tq_{i+1}$: writing a reduced factorization $q_{i+1}=x r_j y$, the product $r_jq_{i+1}=(r_jxr_j)y$ uses one fewer reflections, and the triangle inequality makes that expression reduced. Put $v=q_{i+1}^{-1}c$. Complement order reversal from step 1.5 gives $v\\le_Tr_jc$, so $\\mu(a_j)\\in F(r_jc)\\subseteq F(v)$ for every $j>i$. These $m-i$ independent vectors form a basis of $F(v)$, whose dimension is $m-i$ by Carter's formula. The assumed zero pairings give $a_i\\in F(v)^\\perp=M(v)$. The Wall restriction for the line $\\mathbb Ra_i$ gives $r_i\\le_Tv$; writing $v=r_i z$ then gives $c=q_{i+1}r_i z$, with lengths adding, so $q_i=q_{i+1}r_i\\le_Tc$ and $\\ell_T(q_i)=m-i+1$. At $i=1$ this proves the claimed equivalence. The same argument applies to every subtuple because the vanishing conditions are inherited.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 1.5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $\\rho_k=\\sum_{r=1}^m a_r\\rho_{i_r}$ with $a_r\\ge0$ and $i_1<\\cdots<i_m<k$, then (2)(d) gives $\\mu_k\\cdot\\rho_{i_r}\\le0$ for every $r$, so $1=\\mu_k\\cdot\\rho_k=\\sum_r a_r(\\mu_k\\cdot\\rho_{i_r})\\le0$, a contradiction. This proves (3).",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "Put $E=M(\\sigma)$ and $F=E^\\perp$. Then $W_\\sigma=\\{w:M(w)\\subseteq E\\}$ is the pointwise stabilizer of $F$, because $M(w)=F(w)^\\perp$. If $F\\ne0$, choose $x\\in F$ outside every $F\\cap H_\\alpha$ for which $F\\not\\subseteq H_\\alpha$; this is possible by [F9] and [F12], and $x\\ne0$. By [F10], $\\operatorname{Stab}_W(x)=gW_Ig^{-1}$ for some $g,I$. Each conjugate simple generator has a root hyperplane containing $x$, hence, by the choice of $x$, containing all of $F$; therefore every generator of this stabilizer fixes $F$ pointwise. Conversely every element fixing $F$ fixes $x$, so $W_\\sigma=\\operatorname{Stab}_W(x)=gW_Ig^{-1}$. If $F=0$, then $W_\\sigma=W$. By step 2.2 the roots of this finite parabolic subgroup are exactly $\\pm P_\\sigma$, and they span $E$. Choose $f\\in\\mathcal C^\\circ$ and project it orthogonally to $E$; the projection has positive pairing with every root of $P_\\sigma$, so it lies in a chamber of the parabolic arrangement. The chamber is simplicial by [F10]; its inward unit root normals form a linearly independent set $\\Delta$ of size $k=\\dim E$, and every root in $P_\\sigma$ is a nonnegative linear combination of these normals. Thus $\\Delta$ is a simple system as claimed.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F12",
            "step 2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "In any such positive root subsystem with simple system $\\Gamma$, its first root in the global order is a member of $\\Gamma$. For if the first root $\\eta$ were not simple, write $\\eta=\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\Gamma}b_\\gamma\\gamma$ with $b_\\gamma\\ge0$; each $\\gamma$ is later than $\\eta$, so (2)(d) gives $\\mu(\\gamma)\\cdot\\eta\\le0$. Linearity of $\\mu$ and (1) would give $1=\\mu(\\eta)\\cdot\\eta=\\sum b_\\gamma(\\mu(\\gamma)\\cdot\\eta)\\le0$, impossible. Also the last root of $P_\\sigma$ belongs to $\\Delta$: otherwise it is a nonnegative combination of the earlier simple roots, contrary to step 3.1.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "We prove the recursive selection and the factorization $\\sigma=R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_k)$ by induction on $k$. For $k=1$, $W_\\sigma$ has rank one, $P_\\sigma$ has its single positive root $\\delta_1$, and $\\sigma=R(\\delta_1)$. For $k>1$, order the simple roots increasingly; step 4.1 makes the last one $\\delta_k=\\tau_t$. Put $r=R(\\delta_k)$. Since $r\\le_T\\sigma$, write $\\sigma=ru$ with $\\ell_T(u)=k-1$; then $\\sigma':=\\sigma r=rur$ has length $k-1$. Write $c=\\sigma v$ with $\\ell_T(v)=n-k$. The identity $c=\\sigma'(rv)$ and the bounds $\\ell_T(rv)\\le n-k+1$ and $n=\\ell_T(c)\\le\\ell_T(\\sigma')+\\ell_T(rv)$ force $\\ell_T(rv)=n-k+1$, so $\\sigma'\\le_Tc$. Also $\\sigma'$ fixes $F$ pointwise, hence $M(\\sigma')\\subseteq E$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 3.2",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Let $q$ be the global index with $\\delta_k=\\rho_q$ and put $\\nu=C\\mu_q=\\mu_{q+n}$. For every $\\rho_j\\in P_\\sigma$ one has $j\\le q$ and, by (2)(a) and $C$-equivariance, $\\nu\\cdot\\rho_j=\\mu_{q+n}\\cdot\\rho_j=-\\mu_{j+n}\\cdot\\rho_{q+n}=-\\mu_j\\cdot\\rho_q\\le0$ by (2)(b); at $j=q$ this is $-1$. Since $\\mu_q\\in F(r c)$, conjugating by $C$ gives $\\nu\\in F(c r)$; and $\\ell_T(cr)=n-1$, so $F(cr)=\\mathbb R\\nu$ and $M(cr)=\\nu^\\perp$. Moreover $\\sigma'\\le_Tc r$: its complement is $\\sigma'^{-1}cr=rvr$, of length $n-k$, while $\\ell_T(cr)=n-1$; the lengths add to $n-1$. Hence $U:=M(\\sigma')$ is contained in $E\\cap\\nu^\\perp$, and both have dimension $k-1$ because $\\nu\\cdot\\delta_k=-1$. Thus $U=E\\cap\\nu^\\perp$. The roots $P_{\\sigma'}=\\Phi_+\\cap U$ span $U$ by step 2.2. Each is a nonnegative combination of $\\Delta$ and is orthogonal to $\\nu$; since $\\nu\\cdot\\delta_k=-1$ and $\\nu\\cdot\\delta_j\\le0$ for every $j$, every root in $P_{\\sigma'}$ is a combination only of the $\\delta_j$ for which $\\nu\\cdot\\delta_j=0$. Those zero-pairing simple roots must span the $(k-1)$-space $U$, so they are exactly $\\delta_1,\\dots,\\delta_{k-1}$. They form a simple system for $P_{\\sigma'}$. Induction gives $\\sigma'=R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_{k-1})$ and proves the reverse selection at each rank. Therefore $\\sigma=R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_k)$. For each $i$, its prefix $p_i:=R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_i)$ and complementary suffix $R(\\delta_{i+1})\\cdots R(\\delta_k)$ multiply to $\\sigma$ and have at most $i$ and $k-i$ reflections, so both lengths attain these bounds. Thus $M(p_i)$, contained in $\\operatorname{span}(\\delta_1,\\dots,\\delta_i)$, has dimension $i$ and equals that span. Since $\\sigma R(\\delta_k)\\cdots R(\\delta_{i+1})=p_i$, the induction shows that $\\delta_i$ is the last root of $P_\\sigma$ in this subspace.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "The other recursive description follows by considering the suffix $q_i:=R(\\delta_i)\\cdots R(\\delta_k)$. The factorization in step 6.1 gives $\\ell_T(q_i)=k-i+1$: its prefix and suffix factors together multiply to $\\sigma$ of length $k$, and their lengths are bounded by their numbers of reflections, whose sum is $k$. Writing $p_i:=R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_{i-1})$, the same length equality forces $\\ell_T(p_i)=i-1$, so $q_i^{-1}\\sigma=q_i^{-1}p_iq_i$ has length $i-1$ and $q_i\\le_T\\sigma$. If $c=\\sigma v$ as above, then $q_i^{-1}c=q_i^{-1}(R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_{i-1}))q_i v$ has length at most $(i-1)+(n-k)=n-\\ell_T(q_i)$; the triangle inequality forces equality, so $q_i\\le_Tc$. Its moved space is $\\operatorname{span}(\\delta_i,\\dots,\\delta_k)$, since its factors fix the orthogonal complement of that span and its moved dimension is $k-i+1$. Thus $P_{q_i}=P_\\sigma\\cap M(q_i)$, and $\\{\\delta_i,\\dots,\\delta_k\\}$ is a simple system for this subsystem: a root in the tail span has zero coefficients on $\\delta_1,\\dots,\\delta_{i-1}$ in its nonnegative $\\Delta$-expansion. By step 4.1 the first root of $P_{q_i}$ is a simple root of this tail system; its first simple root is $\\delta_i$. Finally $R(\\delta_{i-1})\\cdots R(\\delta_1)\\sigma=q_i$ by cancellation, so $\\delta_i$ is the first root of $P_\\sigma$ in the moved space in (4)(i), and $\\delta_1=\\tau_1$. This proves (4)(i).",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-2",
          "claim": "Put $D_i:=R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_{i-1})$ and $\\varepsilon_i:=D_i\\delta_i$. The prefix $R(\\delta_1)\\cdots R(\\delta_i)$ has reflection length $i$ by the factorization of $\\sigma$ and the triangle inequality, so this simple-generator word in the Coxeter system of $W_\\sigma$ is reduced. The root-inversion formula [F13], applied to that parabolic system, gives $\\varepsilon_i\\in P_\\sigma$, hence it is a positive root. Conjugation gives $R(\\varepsilon_i)=D_iR(\\delta_i)D_i^{-1}$; multiplying these identities and cancelling adjacent inverse prefixes yields $\\sigma=R(\\varepsilon_k)\\cdots R(\\varepsilon_1)$. Also $R(\\varepsilon_i)D_iR(\\delta_{i+1})\\cdots R(\\delta_k)=D_iR(\\delta_i)R(\\delta_{i+1})\\cdots R(\\delta_k)=\\sigma$. This proves (4)(ii).",
          "step": "7.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F13",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-3",
          "claim": "Suppose $\\ell_T(\\sigma)=2$ and $P_\\sigma=\\{\\tau_1<\\cdots<\\tau_t\\}$. By (4)(i), $\\Delta=\\{\\tau_1,\\tau_t\\}$ and $\\sigma=R(\\tau_1)R(\\tau_t)$. The rank-two parabolic is dihedral: its $t$ positive root rays are equally spaced in the sector bounded by the simple-root rays, whose angle is $\\pi-\\pi/t$. The global order on these roots agrees with their angular order: if $r\\ge2$ and $\\tau_{r+1}$ had smaller angle than $\\tau_r$, it would lie in the positive cone on the earlier roots $\\tau_1,\\tau_r$, contradicting step 3.1; for $r=1$ such a decrease is impossible because $\\tau_1$ is a boundary ray. The product of two reflections in this plane is the rotation through twice the difference of their axis angles. Thus the factorizations of the Coxeter rotation $\\sigma$ into two reflections are exactly $R(\\tau_1)R(\\tau_t)=R(\\tau_2)R(\\tau_1)=\\cdots=R(\\tau_t)R(\\tau_{t-1})$. These are all factorizations in $W$: if $\\sigma=R(\\alpha)R(\\beta)$, then $M(\\sigma)\\subseteq\\operatorname{span}(\\alpha,\\beta)$, and both spaces have dimension $2$, so $\\alpha,\\beta\\in M(\\sigma)$ and their positive representatives lie in $P_\\sigma$ by step 2.2. The endpoint angle gives $\\tau_1\\cdot\\tau_t=-\\cos(\\pi/t)\\le0$ and each adjacent angle gives $\\tau_i\\cdot\\tau_{i+1}=\\cos(\\pi/t)\\ge0$.",
